\begin{proof}
  See Appendix~\ref{apx:property of SNFGs}.
\end{proof}

We obtain the following observations on Proposition~\ref{sec:LCT:prop:1}.
%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{itemize}

    \item Property~\ref{sec:LCT:prop:1:item:2} states that the LCT is a reparamerization of the global function based on an SPA fixed point $ \vmu $ such that 
    the global function $\LCT{g}$ evaluated at $ \LCTv{x} = \vect{0} $ equals the Bethe partition function evaluated at $ \vmu $. Combining with Property~\ref{sec:LCT:prop:1:item:1}, the LCT can be viewed as shifting the bulk of the summation in the partition function to some known configuration while keeping the partition function unchanged.

    \item Property~\ref{sec:LCT:prop:1:item:6} highlights that  $\LCT{\graphN}$ has an SPA fixed-point message vector $ \LCT{\vmu} $ with a simple structure,
     % Specifically, the messages $ \LCT{\vmu} $ satisfy $ \ZBSPA( \graphN, \vmu ) = \ZBSPA( \LCT{\graphN}, \LCT{\vmu} ) $ and $\LCT{\mu}_{\efi}(\LCT{x}_e) = \LCT{\mu}_{\efj}(\LCT{x}_e) = [\LCT{x}_e \! = \! 0] $ for all $ \LCT{x}_{e} \in \LCTset{X}_{e} $ and $ e = (f_{i}, f_{j}) \in \setEfull $. 
     which is the crucial ingredient in the proof of the graph-cover theorem (Thereom~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1}).

\end{itemize}
