      $f_{j}$ in the definition of $M_{\efi}$ leads to the definition of
      $M_{\efj}$, and vice-versa.

      \item Assume that $M_{\efi}$ and $M_{\efj}$ are defined based on the
      choices $\zeta_{\efi} = \zeta_{\efj} \defeq Z_e^{-1/2}$,
      $\chi_{\efi} = \chi_{\efj} \defeq 1$,
      $\delta_{\efi} = \delta_{\efj} \defeq Z_e^{1/2}$. 
      Let
      $e = (f_{i},f_{j}) \in \setE$ be an edge such that
      $\mu_{\efi}(\txe) = \overline{\mu_{\efj}(\txe)}$ for all $\txe \in \tset{X}_e$. 
      In this case, both $ \matr{M}_{\efi} $ and $ \matr{M}_{\efj} $ are unitary matrices and satisfy
      $ \matr{M}_{\efi} = \overline{\matr{M}_{\efj}} $.
    \end{enumerate}

    \item\label{prop:DENFG:LCT:1:item:8} For each $ e = (f_{i}, f_{j}) $, it holds that 
    $ \matr{ C }_{ M_{\efi} },\matr{ C }_{ M_{\efi} } \in \setHerm{ \setxe } $.

    \item\label{prop:DENFG:LCT:1:item:9} For each $f \in \setF$, 
    it holds that $\matr{C}_{\LCT{f}} \in \setHerm{ \LCTset{X}_{\setpf} } $.
      % where $ \matr{C}_{\LCT{f}} $ is defined to be a matrix with row indices $ \LCTv{x}_{\setpf} $ and column indices $ \LCTv{x}_{\setpf}' $ such that
      % %-------------------------------------------------------------------
      % \begin{align}
      %     \matr{C}_{\LCT{f}} \defeq 
      %     \Bigl( \LCT{f}(\LCTv{x}_{\setpf}, \LCTv{x}_{\setpf}') 
      %     \Bigr)_{ (\LCTv{x}_{\setpf}, \LCTv{x}_{\setpf}') 
      %     \in \LCTtset{X}_{\setpf} }.
      %     \label{sec:LCT:eqn:6}
      % \end{align}
      % %-------------------------------------------------------------------

    \item\label{LCT on strict sense DENFG results in weak sense DENFG} 
    The resulting NFG $\LCT{\graphN}$ is a weak-sense DE-NFG. 
    (Recall that strict-sense DE-NFG and 
    weak-sense DE-NFG
    are defined in Definition~\ref{sec:DENFG:def:4}.
    In Assumption~\ref{sec:DENFG:asum:2}, we impose the condition that the original DE-NFG
    $ \sfN $ is a strict-sense DE-NFG.)

  \end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
  See Appendix~\ref{apx:LCT DENFGs}.
\end{proof}


From Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1}, we can see that most properties of the LCT for DE-NFG are natural extensions of those established for S-NFG. In particular, Property~\ref{LCT on strict sense DENFG results in weak sense DENFG} in Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1} closely parallels the corresponding property of the LCT for S-NFG, as discussed in Item~\ref{remk: LCT on snfg results an NFG with negative fun nodes} in Remark~\ref{sec:LCT:rem:1}.

