\subsection{The Average Degree \texorpdfstring{$M$}{}-Cover of a DE-NFG}
\label{sec:sst:DENFG:1}

%***************************************************************************
The definition of the 
average degree-$M$ cover of an arbitrary DE-NFG is a straightforward extension of 
that in the case of S-NFG.
(Recall that in a degree-$M$ cover of a DE-NFG, the double edges are permuted together. 
See Definition~\ref{sec:GraCov:def:1} and Fig.~\ref{fig:Exam_Graph_cover_DE_NFG} for the definition of finite graph cover and the corresponding example in the case of DE-NFG, respectively.) 
Compared with the case of S-NFG, the major change in the case of DE-NFG is that for
every edge $e \in \setEfull$, the alphabet $\set{X}_e$ is replaced with
the alphabet $\tset{X}_e = \set{X}_e \times \set{X}_e$.

Following the similar idea as in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm: expression of Z for degree M Bethe partition function}, 
the expression of
$\ZBM(\graphN)$ for an arbitrary DE-NFG $\graphN$ can be written as
\begin{align}
    \bigl( \ZBM(\graphN) \bigr)^{\! M}
    = 
    \sum_{\vx_{[M]}}
    \Biggl( \prod_{m \in [M]} \prod_f f(\tvx_{\setpff,m}) \Biggr)
    \cdot \prod_{e} P_e\bigl( \tvx_{\efi,[M]}, \tvx_{\efj,[M]} \bigr),
  \label{eqn: Z for degree M average cover of DENFG}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align*}
  P_e\bigl( \tvx_{\efi,[M]}, \tvx_{\efj,[M]} \bigr)
  = \begin{cases}
    |\set{T}_{e,\vt_e}|^{-1}
     &  
     \vt_e = \vt_e\bigl(\tvx_{\efi,[M]}\bigr) 
     = \vt_e\bigl( \tvx_{\efj,[M]} \bigr) \\
   0
     & \text{otherwise}
  \end{cases}. 
  % \label{eqn: expression of Pe for DE-NFG}
\end{align*}

The average degree M-cover construction for S-NFG and DE-NFG motivates us to introduce the symmetric-subspace transform for S-NFG and DE-NFG in~\cite[Chapter 5]{Huang2024}. For any S-NFG or DE-NFG, denoted by $ \graphN $, the symmetric-subspace transform rewrites $ \bigl( \ZBM( \graphN ) \bigr)^{\! M} $ as an integral, offering a novel perspective on the degree $M$-cover of $ \graphN $. Although widely employed in quantum physics~\cite{Wood2015,Harrow2013}, its application to factor-graph literature is, to our knowledge, novel.

