Consider a DE-NFG $\graphN$. The setup of our approach is as follows.

Assume that among all SPA fixed-point message vectors for $\graphN$, we obtain the vector $\vmu$ that achieves the SPA-based Bethe partition function, \ie,
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{align}
   \ZBSPA(\graphN,\vmu) = \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN) \in \sR_{>0}, \qquad 
   Z_{e}(\vmu) >0, \qquad e \in \setEfull. \label{eqn:positive_ZBSPA}
\end{align}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------
(See also Definition~\ref{sec:DENFG:def:3} and Assumption~\ref{sec:DENFG:remk:1}.) 
Based on $\vmu$, we apply the LCT to $\graphN$ and obtain the loop-calculus transformed NFG $ \LCT{\graphN} $. 
The NFG $ \LCT{\graphN} $ has the following properties, as proven in Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1}.
%----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{enumerate}

    \item The partition functions of $ \graphN $ and 
   $ \LCT{\graphN} $ are equal, \ie, $ Z\bigl( \LCT{\graphN} \bigr) = Z(\graphN) $.

    \item For any $ M \in \sZpp $, the degree-$M$ Bethe partition functions of $ \graphN $ and 
   $ \LCT{\graphN} $ are equal, \ie,
   %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
   \begin{align*}
      \ZBM(\graphN) 
      &\overset{(a)}{=} \sqrt[M]{
       \bigl\langle
          Z\bigl( \hgraphN \bigr)
        \bigr\rangle_{ \hgraphN \in \hat{\set{N}}_{M}}
      }
      \overset{(b)}{=} \sqrt[M]{
         \Bigl\langle
             Z\bigl( \hLCTgraphN \bigr)
         \Bigr\rangle_{ \hLCTgraphN \in \hLCT{\set{N}}_{M} }
      }
      \overset{(c)}{=} \ZBM\bigl( \LCT{\graphN} \bigr) ,\qquad M \in \sZpp,  \nonumber
   \end{align*}
   %----------------------------------------------------------------------
   where step $(a)$ follows from the definition of the function $ \ZBM $ in Definition~\ref{sec:GraCov:def:2}, where step $(b)$ follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1},
   and where step $(c)$ again follows from~Definition~\ref{sec:GraCov:def:2}.

    \item The  SPA fixed-point message vector $\vmu$ induces an SPA fixed-point message vector
     $\LCTv{\mu} \defeq \bigl( \LCTv{\mu}_{\ef} \bigr)_{ \! e \in \setpf,\, f \in \setF}$ for
     $\LCT{\graphN}$ with a simple structure
     %-------------------------------------------------------------------
     \begin{align*}
         \LCT{\mu}_{e,f}(\LCTt{x}_{e})
         &= \bigl[ \LCTt{x}_{e} \! = \! \tzero \bigr],
         \qquad
         \LCTt{x}_{e} \in \LCTtset{X}_{e}, \,
         e \in \setpf, \, f \in \setF.
     \end{align*}
     %-------------------------------------------------------------------
     Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1} implies that
     %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
     \begin{align}
         \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN)
         % = \ZBSPA^{*}\bigl( \LCT{\graphN} \bigr)
         = \ZBSPA(\graphN,\vmu)
         = \ZBSPA(\LCT{\graphN}, \LCTv{\mu})
         =
         \prod_{f} \LCT{f}(\tv{0}).
         \label{sec:CheckCon:eqn:31}
     \end{align}
     % where the inequality follows from~\eqref{eqn:positive_ZBSPA}.
\end{enumerate}
%----------------------------------------------------------------------------

For any $ M \in \sZpp $, we consider an arbitrary degree-$M$ cover of $ \LCT{\graphN} $, which is denoted by $ \hLCTgraphN $. This graph cover is specified by a collection of permutations 
$ \vsigma = (\sigma_e)_{e \in \setEfull} 
\in \set{S}^{|\setEfull|}_{M} $.
The following vectors 
\begin{align*}
   \hat{\LCTv{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{i},m)} 
   \defeq \bigl( \hat{\LCT{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{i},m)}(\LCTt{x}_{e}) 
   \bigr)_{\! \LCTt{x}_{e} \in \LCTtset{X}_{e}}, \qquad 
   \hat{\LCTv{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{j},\sigma_{e}(m))} 
   \defeq \bigl( \hat{\LCT{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{j},\sigma_{e}(m))}(\LCTt{x}_{e}) 
   \bigr)_{\! \LCTt{x}_{e} \in \LCTtset{X}_{e}},
   \qquad 
   \, m \in [M],\, e = (f_{i},f_{j}) \in \setEfull,
\end{align*}
whose elements are specified by
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{align*}
  \hat{\LCT{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{i},m)}(\LCTt{x}_{e})=
  \hat{\LCT{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f_{j},\sigma_{e}(m))}(\LCTt{x}_{e})
  = \bigl[ \LCTt{x}_{e} \! = \! \tzero \bigr],
  \qquad 
  \LCTt{x}_{e} \in \LCTtset{X}_{e},
\end{align*}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------
form an SPA fixed-point message vector for $ \hLCTgraphN $.
For each pair $(f,m') \in \{ (f_{i},m), (f_{j}, \sigma_{e}(m)) \}$, each integer $ m \in [M] $, and each edge $ e=(f_{i},f_{j}) \in \setEfull $, the vector $ \hat{\LCTv{\mu}}_{(e,m),(f,m')} $ corresponds to the SPA fixed-point message from edge $ (e,m) $ to local function $ (f,m') $ in $ \hLCTgraphN $. 
(Because the $M$-cover $\hLCTgraphN$ looks locally the same as the base graph $\LCT{\graphN}$, the SPA fixed-point message vector $ \LCTv{\mu}$ for $\LCT{\graphN}$ yields an SPA fixed-point message vector $\hat{\LCTv{\mu}}$ for $\hLCTgraphN$ simply by repeating every message $M$ times.\footnote{This observation was the original motivation for the graph cover analysis of message-passing iterative decoders~\cite{Koetter2003,Koetter2007}.}) 
Similar to the idea in Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1}, the partition function $Z\bigl( \hLCTgraphN \bigr)$ has a property that the contribution of the all-zero configuration is $ \ZBSPA(\hLCTgraphN, \hat{\LCTv{\mu}}) = \bigl( \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN) \bigr)^{M}$, \ie, the
$M$-th power the SPA-based Bethe partition function of the base graph, and all other configurations correspond to generalized loops in $\hLCTgraphN$. 
(Note that the order of operations, namely constructing a graph cover and applying the LCT, does not affect the resulting partition function $Z\bigl( \hLCTgraphN \bigr)$. In other words, changing the order of these two operations does not alter the outcome.)

In order to prove the graph-cover theorem for $\sfN$, \ie, proving Conjecture~\ref{sec:GraCov:conj:1} for $\sfN$, we analyze the average degree-$M$ cover $ \hat{\LCT{\graphN}}_{M,\mathrm{avg}} $ whose partition function is
$ Z(\hat{\LCT{\graphN}}_{M,\mathrm{avg}}) =  \langle
             Z\bigl( \hLCTgraphN \bigr)
         \rangle_{ \hLCTgraphN \in \hLCT{\set{N}}_{M} } $, where $\hLCT{\set{N}}_{M}$ is the set of all $M$-covers of $ \LCT{\graphN} $. We then evaluate 
$ \ZBM\bigl( \LCT{\graphN} \bigr) = \sqrt[M]{ Z(\hat{\LCT{\graphN}}_{M,\mathrm{avg}}) } $ as $ M \to \infty $.
Now we present a checkable sufficient condition for the case where the limit $ \lim\limits_{M \to \infty} \ZBM\bigl( \LCT{\graphN} \bigr) = \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN)$, \ie, the graph cover theorem for $\sfN$, holds.
%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{theorem}\label{sec:CheckCon:thm:1}
  Consider a DE-NFG $ \graphN $, along with the associated loop-calculus transformed NFG $ \LCT{\graphN} $. If 
  the checkable inequality
  %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  \begin{align}
    \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN)
    &>
    \frac{2}{3}
    \cdot 
    \prod_{f}
     \left( 
        \sum_{\LCTtv{x}_{\setpf}}
        \bigl| \LCT{f}( \LCTtv{x}_{\setpf} ) \bigr|
    \right)
    % \Biggr),
    % \sum_{ \LCTvxs, \LCTvxl }
    % \bigl|G_{ \LCTzero{\graphN}, 1 }( \LCTvxs, \LCTvxl )\bigr|,
    \label{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70}
  \end{align}
  holds,
  %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  then we have
  %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  \begin{align*}
    \lim_{M \to \infty}
    \ZBM(\graphN)
    = \lim_{M \to \infty}
    \ZBM(\LCT{\graphN})
    = \ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN). 
  \end{align*}
  %-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  % {\color{red}(whether $\limsup$ or $ \liminf $, we will come back later)}
\end{theorem}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{proof}
  See Appendix~\ref{apx:check_graph_thm}.
\end{proof}
%---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1} provides the first combinatorial characterization of the SPA-based Bethe partition function for a special class of DE-NFGs. This result elevates the SPA-based Bethe approximation for this class of DE-NFGs from a heuristic to a quantity with a rigorous combinatorial interpretation, mirroring the established understanding for S-NFGs in the classical case. The condition in~\eqref{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70}, while technical, is the critical element that makes this proof possible, and its role warrants a detailed discussion.

\subsection{Discussion of the Condition in \texorpdfstring{Theorem~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1}}{Theorem 46}}

The condition presented in~\eqref{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70} is the central assumption that enables our proof strategy. It provides a sufficient, easily checkable criterion for the graph-cover theorem to hold.

\subsubsection{Interpretation of the Condition}

The condition in~\eqref{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70} imposes a constraint on the magnitudes of the local functions in the loop-calculus transformed NFG $\LCT{\graphN}$. As established in Proposition~\ref{prop:DENFG:LCT:1}, the LCT reparameterizes the partition function such that the contribution of the all-zero configuration, $\LCT{g}(\tv{0})$, equals the SPA-based Bethe partition function, $\ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN)$. All other configurations, $\LCTtv{x} \neq \tv{0}$, correspond to generalized loops, and their contributions, $\LCT{g}(\LCTtv{x}) = \prod_{f}\LCT{f}(\LCTtv{x}_{\setpf})$, constitute complex-valued correction terms. The condition~\eqref{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70} bounds the collective weight of all generalized loop configurations relative to the weight of the dominant, all-zero configuration.

\subsubsection{Role in the Proof Methodology}

This dominance condition is essential for the proof technique detailed in Appendix~\ref{apx:check_graph_thm}. The proof strategy expands the partition function of the average degree-$M$ cover, $Z(\hat{\LCT{\graphN}}_{M,\mathrm{avg}}) = \bigl( \ZBM(\graphN) \bigr)^{\!M}$, into a series where the leading term corresponds to $(\ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN))^{M}$ and subsequent correction terms represent contributions from the generalized loops in $\hat{\LCT{\graphN}}_{M,\mathrm{avg}}$. The core of the proof is to show that, as $M \to \infty$, the sum of these correction terms becomes negligible compared to the leading term.

For DE-NFGs, these correction terms are complex-valued, and their summation involves intricate cancellations, a manifestation of the numerical sign problem that complicates direct analysis~\cite{LohJr1990}. The inequality in~\eqref{sec:CheckCon:eqn:70} provides the analytical leverage to bound the magnitude of the sum of all correction terms. It ensures that the geometric series formed by these correction terms converges, as $M \to \infty$, allowing the limit of $\ZBM(\graphN)$ to be controlled by $\ZBSPA^{*}(\graphN)$.

\subsubsection{Physical and Structural Implications}

The condition in Theorem~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1} relates to the physical properties of the system modeled by the DE-NFG. Systems likely to satisfy the condition are those where the SPA-based Bethe approximation is already expected to be highly accurate. These systems are typically characterized by weak correlations, such as DE-NFGs where the Choi matrix for each local function is nearly proportional to the identity matrix or is strongly diagonally dominant. In physical terms, this describes systems with weak interactions or low entanglement between local degrees of freedom. In such cases, the SPA messages converge to a solution that accurately captures the system's state, and the loop corrections are naturally small. The LCT then serves to isolate this already-dominant contribution.

\subsubsection{Scope and Generality of the Result}

Theorem~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1} rigorously establishes the graph-cover theorem for a well-defined, non-trivial class of DE-NFGs. While the condition may appear restrictive, it defines a region of the problem space where the LCT-based approach is provably effective. The numerical results in Examples~\ref{sec:GraCov:exp:1} and~\ref{sec:GraCov:exp:2} suggest that the graph-cover theorem holds more broadly, even for randomly generated DE-NFGs that were not explicitly checked against this condition. We therefore believe the theorem's condition is sufficient but not necessary. Proving Conjecture~\ref{sec:GraCov:conj:1} for general DE-NFGs remains a key open problem, a point we elaborate on in the following section.

\section{Conclusion and Open Problems}
\label{sec: conclusion and open problems}

This paper establishes a graph-cover-based characterization of the Bethe partition function for a class of DE-NFGs. This result contributes to the study of graphical models with complex-valued functions, which are of significant relevance to quantum information processing and the analysis of tensor networks~\cite{Cao2017,Alkabetz2021,Cao2021,Cirac2009,Coecke2010,Robeva2019,Coecke2008,Coecke2011}. By proving that the limit of the degree-$M$ Bethe partition function equals the SPA-based Bethe partition function for DE-NFGs satisfying an easily checkable condition, we establish a combinatorial foundation for the Bethe approximation in this setting.

The proof relies on a generalized LCT that, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:lct_comparison}, is explicit for non-binary alphabets and accommodates the zero-valued SPA fixed-point messages that can arise in DE-NFGs. This generalization extends the applicability of loop calculus techniques beyond prior work that required strictly positive messages~\cite{Mori2015}. These results help to explain the performance of the SPA for approximating tensor network contractions by connecting it to the combinatorial properties of the underlying graph.

Despite this progress, several fundamental questions remain open, defining a clear path for future research.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The general graph-cover conjecture:} The primary open problem is to prove or disprove Conjecture~\ref{sec:GraCov:conj:1}, which posits that the graph-cover theorem holds for all DE-NFGs, not just those satisfying the condition in Theorem~\ref{sec:CheckCon:thm:1}. The numerical evidence presented in Section~\ref{sec:GraCov} supports this conjecture. A proof of this general result would be a significant milestone, fully equating the SPA-based Bethe approximation with its combinatorial graph-cover counterpart for all DE-NFGs.

    \item \textbf{Overcoming the numerical sign problem in the limit:} The central technical barrier to proving the general conjecture is analyzing the limit of the average degree $M$-cover partition function when the sum over generalized loop configurations involves significant destructive interference. This is the numerical sign problem that complicates many quantum and statistical physics models. New analytical techniques are required to manage these complex-valued sums in the limit.

    \item \textbf{A promising path forward:} A promising avenue for future investigation is the \textbf{symmetric-subspace transform}, introduced in~\cite[Chapter 5]{Huang2024}. This transform reformulates the average degree $M$-cover partition function in terms of an integral. This integral representation may provide the analytical insights needed to analyze the complex-valued sum in the general case, potentially by leveraging tools from random matrix theory or Laplace's method.

    \item \textbf{Analysis of correction terms:} A second open problem is to explore the utility of the generalized LCT for systematically analyzing the correction terms to the Bethe approximation, similar to the loop series expansion for S-NFGs. Answering this could lead to systematically improvable approximations for DE-NFGs' partition functions and provide a method for high-precision calculations in quantum simulation.

    \item \textbf{The primal formulation of Bethe free energy function:} Finally, extending the primal formulation of the Bethe free energy function (Definition~\ref{sec:SNFG:def:1}) from S-NFG to DE-NFG remains a challenge due to the multi-valued nature of the complex logarithm. Resolving this issue could provide a variational perspective on the Bethe approximation for DE-NFG that complements the combinatorial approach, potentially opening new algorithmic and analytical pathways.
\end{itemize}

Addressing these questions will deepen the theoretical understanding of graphical models and may enhance the ability to analyze and simulate complex quantum systems.


