\begin{abstract}
Understanding how complex societal behaviors emerge from individual cognition and interactions requires both high-fidelity modeling of human behavior and large-scale simulations. Traditional agent-based models (ABMs) have been employed to study these dynamics for decades, but are constrained by simplified agent behaviors that fail to capture human complexity. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities by enabling agents to exhibit sophisticated social behaviors that go beyond rule-based logic, yet face significant scaling challenges.
%
Here we present Light Society, an agent-based simulation framework that advances both fronts, efficiently modeling human-like societies at planetary scale powered by LLMs. Light Society formalizes social processes as structured transitions of agent and environment states, governed by a set of LLM-powered simulation operations, and executed through an event queue. This modular design supports both independent and joint component optimization, supporting efficient simulation of societies with over one billion agents.
%
Large-scale simulations of trust games and opinion propagation—spanning up to one billion agents—demonstrate Light Society’s high fidelity and efficiency in modeling social trust and information diffusion, while revealing scaling laws whereby larger simulations yield more stable and realistic emergent behaviors.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

The past decade has witnessed remarkable advancements in artificial intelligence, culminating in large language models (LLMs) that not only demonstrate unprecedented capabilities in language understanding and generation~\cite{openai2024gpt4technicalreport,brown2020languagemodelsfewshotlearners,zhao2025surveylargelanguagemodels}, but also achieve near-human performance on complex tasks requiring advanced reasoning including mathematics and coding~\cite{deepseekai2025deepseekr1incentivizingreasoningcapability,openai2025competitiveprogramminglargereasoning}. These capabilities have catalyzed the emergence of LLM-powered autonomous agents that are capable of perceiving their environment, making decisions, and taking actions in complex, dynamic settings~\cite{xi2023risepotentiallargelanguage,Wang_2024,Boiko2023}. Crucially, such agents can emulate nuanced individual behaviors—maintaining personality traits~\cite{huang2024on,miotto-etal-2022-gpt,chen2024personapersonalizationsurveyroleplaying}, exhibiting self-awareness~\cite{li2024ithinkiam,BLUM202312}, and expressing context-sensitive emotions~\cite{huang2024emotionallynumbempatheticevaluating,10.19066/COGSCI.2024.35.1.002}—capturing the fluidity and depth of human-like reactions. With the ability to simulate realistic and coherent individual behaviors, LLM-powered agents offer a new tool for investigating cognition, personality, and decision-making across diverse and reproducible scenarios.

Social science, as the study of human behavior in social contexts, offers key insights into how societies function and evolve. Traditional methods—such as questionnaires~\cite{foddy1993constructing,behr2018introduction}, interviews~\cite{Knott2022,roulston2003learning}, and controlled experiments~\cite{andreoni2021predicting,dehue2001establishing}—have long provided valuable insights, yet they are often costly, ethically constrained, and difficult to scale or replicate. Agent-based modeling (ABM) emerged as a computational alternative, enabling in silico experiments on social phenomena by simulating interactions among rule-based agents~\cite{axtell2025agent,axtell2016120,aylett2021june,aylett2022epidemiological,ghaffarian2021agent}. However, most ABMs rely on heuristics or fixed equations, limiting their capacity to reflect the complexity and adaptability of real-world societies~\cite{bonabeau2002agent}. The rise of large language models offers a transformative opportunity: agents powered by LLMs can generate context-rich, expressive, and adaptive behaviors, enabling simulations that integrate cognitive, emotional, and social dimensions. Recent work has demonstrated the promise of such agents in reproducing social dynamics like herd behavior~\cite{liu2024lmagent,yang2024oasis}, cooperation~\cite{guo2024embodied,vallinder2024cultural}, polarization~\cite{yang2024oasis,piao2025agentsociety}, information spreading~\cite{piao2025agentsociety,yang2024oasis}, presidential election~\cite{zhang2025socioverse,yu2024will}, and the impact of disease outbreaks~\cite{chopra2024limits}, suggesting that LLM-powered simulation may bridge longstanding gaps between computational models and the fluid realities of human societies.

Despite recent advances, current LLM-powered simulation frameworks remain prohibitively costly. For instance, many existing studies require dozens of GPUs and weeks of computation to simulate up to a million agents in social scenarios~\cite{yang2024oasis,piao2025agentsociety,liu2024lmagent,zhang2025socioverse,wang2025yulanonesimgenerationsocialsimulator,gao2024agentscopeflexiblerobustmultiagent,tang2024gensimgeneralsocialsimulation}. As simulations scale up, storage demands, latency, and overall computational costs grow rapidly, often rendering planet-scale simulations infeasible. Consequently, when attempting to study complex global phenomena, scaling simulations to a global level while maintaining high fidelity becomes challenging and expensive. In addition, many existing frameworks are task-specific and lack generalizability~\cite{yang2024oasis,liu2024lmagent,ren2024baseslargescalewebsearch,mou2024unveilingtruthfacilitatingchange,zhang2024largescaletimeawareagentssimulation}, limiting their applicability across diverse social simulation conditions.

To advance the field of simulating large-scale societal dynamics with cognitively rich agents, we introduce Light Society, a modular simulation framework powered by LLMs capable of simulating societies with over one billion agents. At its core, Light Society captures social evolution by encoding interactions as transitions between agent and environment states, triggered by LLM-powered simulation operations and paced by an event queue, with all components carefully designed to preserve the complexity of real-world societies. This design enables not only high-fidelity modeling of human social behaviors, but also efficient simulation by decoupling the simulation system into modular components that can be optimized both independently and jointly.

To accelerate large-scale simulation, Light Society integrates a suite of algorithmic and system-level optimizations. At the inference level, semantic prompt caching identifies and reuses structurally similar prompts during the simulation, significantly reducing redundant LLM queries. To further reduce computational cost, we apply knowledge distillation, training lightweight surrogate models on task-specific distributions to approximate the outputs of LLMs. A mixture-of-models architecture, with automated load balancing across heterogeneous inference backends—including LLMs, surrogate models, and models served by different providers—enables flexible trade-offs between fidelity and efficiency. Parallelized inference pipelines further improve throughput by distributing simulation tasks across diverse model types and infrastructure pathways. At the system level, Light Society employs a distributed execution engine and high-performance storage to coordinate simulation at scale, and compressed graph representations ensure efficient communication across billion-node networks. At the event level, the framework employs an event queue with support for event aggregation—merging concurrent events of the same type to minimize redundant computation—and asynchronous execution, which decouples independent events to maximize concurrency. This modular and composable architecture supports plug-and-play accelerations, allowing domain-specific enhancements to be integrated without altering the core simulation engine.

To demonstrate the capabilities of Light Society, we conduct two large-scale simulation studies. The first examines socio-demographic patterns of trust and reciprocity through a globally instantiated trust game with agents derived from real-world survey data, revealing how social stratification shapes interpersonal behavior and how scaling influences the emergence of stable behavioral norms. The second investigates opinion propagation via influencer–influencee interactions across a one-billion-agent network, capturing how individual views shift through repeated social exposure. This simulation demonstrates the computational efficiency of Light Society at planetary scale, while uncovering propagation dynamics consistent with real-world patterns of information diffusion. Together, these studies highlight the potential of Light Society as a testbed for exploring emergent phenomena, counterfactual interventions, and large-scale modeling across the human sciences.

\section{Light Society: An Efficient LLM-Powered Social Simulation Framework}\label{sec:light-society}

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]{figs/main_fig.pdf}
    \caption{\small \textbf{Agent-based social simulation framework powered by LLMs.} \textbf{a}, Conceptual overview of the social simulation platform capable of modeling various social phenomena including rumor propagation, norm emergence, trust behavior, and herd effects. \textbf{b}, Formal specification of the simulation system, consisting of agents, environment, event queue, and simulation operations. Each agent maintains a static profile, internal status, and external status, while the environment encompasses both static and dynamic components. \textbf{c}, Execution pipeline for simulation operations, using a mixture-of-models architecture with prompt caching and automatic load balancing across heterogeneous inference backends. \textbf{d}, Initialization process using a seed dataset to generate the initial agent states, environment states, and event queue. \textbf{e}, Readout mechanism extracts structured outputs from the evolving agent and environment states along with events, enabling downstream analysis.}
    \label{fig:main}
\end{figure}

To realize large-scale simulation of cognitively rich societies, Light Society is designed as a modular and efficient framework that integrates LLM-powered social cognition, modeling social dynamics as transitions between agent and environment states orchestrated by simulation operations and scheduled through an event queue, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:main}. The model is defined as the tuple:
\begin{align}
M &:= \langle D, T, S_{A}, S_{E}, V, Q, F \rangle \\
F &:= \{ f_{I}, f_{P}, f_{\Pi}, f_{A}, f_{E}, f_{U} \}
\end{align}
Here, $T$ denotes the timeline of the simulation. At each time step, the system maintains a dynamic set of active agents, which may change over time due to internal events such as birth, death, or shifts in participation (for example, when individuals join or leave a social platform). Each agent has a state $S_A$ composed of three elements: a static profile that remains constant throughout the simulation (such as demographics or personality traits), an internal status that evolves over time (capturing aspects like memory, beliefs, and goals), and an external status that reflects observable features like the agent’s location or social connections. The environment state $S_E$ is defined by both static components, such as spatial layout or institutional structures, and dynamic components that change as the simulation progresses—these might include things like weather conditions or disasters.

Events $v \in \mathcal{V}$ in Light Society serve as the primary drivers of simulation dynamics. Each event encapsulates a discrete interaction or change in the system, such as an agent sending a message, relocating, forming a relationship. Events are assigned timestamps for execution in the simulation and may involve one or more initiating entities and one or more target entities (e.g., agents, environmental modules). They carry structured payloads that describe the content and context of the interaction—for instance, the text of a message, a resource transfer, or a behavioral intention. Events may also include a priority level, allowing the system to resolve multiple simultaneous events deterministically. All events are stored in an event queue, which functions as a priority queue sorted by time and priority, ensuring consistent and temporally coherent execution of interactions throughout the simulation.

The core dynamics of Light Society are governed by a collection of simulation operations $F$ powered by large language models. These operations define how agents perceive, reason, act, and evolve over time, as well as how the environment responds and the system state is updated accordingly. At the outset, an initialization operation $f_I$ configures the simulation by generating the initial agent states, the environment state, a set of starting events, based on a seed dataset $D$. During simulation, agents perceive their surroundings via a perception operation $f_P$, make decisions and generate actions using a policy operation $f_{\Pi}$ that produces events. As time progresses, agent and environment states evolve independently through dedicated evolution operations $f_A$ and $f_E$, reflecting internal dynamics such as memory decay or environmental drift. An update operation $f_U$ applies events to the simulation system, ensuring consistent transitions. After the simulation, a readout process aggregates historical system states and events into measurable outputs for downstream analysis. Together, these operations form a flexible and scalable framework for simulating complex social dynamics.

To support efficient inference at planetary scale, Light Society implements a multi-tiered optimization pipeline. At its core is a semantic prompt caching mechanism that reduces redundant LLM invocations by identifying and reusing responses to structurally similar prompts. During simulation, each query to the LLM is first converted into a vector representation and is stored in a vector database that supports fast searching algorithms. When a new prompt is generated, the system performs similarity search in the embedding space to determine whether to reuse a semantically equivalent prompt that has been processed previously. Besides, knowledge distillation is applied to train compact task-specific surrogate models that approximate LLM behavior for routine decisions and environment updates. These models are periodically retrained using live simulation data to adapt to domain drift, maintaining high fidelity while offloading frequent and repetitive tasks from full LLM calls.

Simulation operations are dispatched through a mixture-of-models architecture, allowing tasks to be routed to full LLMs, distilled models, or other inference backends based on fidelity needs and resource availability. Combined with parallelized pipelines, this design maximizes throughput by distributing computation across heterogeneous infrastructure. At the system level, Light Society leverages a distributed execution engine and compressed graph structures to support efficient state updates and neighbor queries across billion-node networks. The event queue supports event aggregation—merging simultaneous events of the same type—and asynchronous execution, enabling concurrent processing of independent interactions and improving system throughput.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

To evaluate the capacity of Light Society to model complex societal behaviors at scale, we conduct two representative simulations emphasizing both expressiveness and efficiency. In section~\ref{sec:trust-game}, we investigate patterns of human trust through agent-based simulations of the trust game. In section \ref{sec:1b}, we study opinion propagation via influencer–influencee interactions across a one-billion-agent network.

\subsection{Trust Game Simulation}\label{sec:trust-game}

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]{figs/trust_game_exp.pdf}
    \caption{\small \textbf{Trust game simulation.} \textbf{a}, Overview of the simulation pipeline. Demographic profiles from the World Values Survey (WVS) are used to instantiate agents in the trust game, simulated via large language models (LLMs). All output decisions are collected for downstream analysis. \textbf{b}, Demographic distribution of the simulated agent population, including country (showing top 10), age group, and subjective social class. \textbf{c}, Trustor transfer behavior stratified by subjective social class and education level across two LLMs (A: \texttt{gemini-2.0-flash-001}, B: \texttt{gpt-4.1-nano}). Color denotes density normalized within each demographic category. \textbf{d}, Trustee return behavior across all received amounts (tripled from trustor transfer), showing average return, trustor net profit, fair return line, and return ratios. \textbf{e}, Mean trustee return amount when trustor sending \$3, stratified by subjective social class (left), urban–rural residence (middle), and education level (right). \textbf{f}, Scaling behavior of the difference in trustor send amounts between the 16–34 and 55+ age groups. As population size increases, stratification in trust behavior becomes more pronounced, with shaded areas indicating 95\% confidence intervals. \textbf{g}, Mean trustor send amount by age group over 8 simulation trials across different population sizes. Trust levels stabilize as population scales up, with younger agents consistently sending more.}
    \label{fig:trust}
\end{figure}

We evaluated how individual trust behavior and reciprocity vary across socio-economic backgrounds using a large-scale simulation of trust games with LLMs. In a trust game, a trustor decides how much of an initial \$10 endowment to send to an anonymous trustee, with the amount tripled upon transfer~\cite{cox2004identify,berg1995trust,xie2024can}. The trustee then decides how much of the received amount to return. This minimal setup captures key dynamics of social exchange, including risk-taking, fairness, and reciprocity.

Our simulation pipeline (Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}a) uses agent profiles constructed from the World Values Survey (WVS) Wave 7~\cite{haerpfer2022world,christian2014world,lopez2023prioritizing,zhao2024worldvaluesbenchlargescalebenchmarkdataset}, which offers extensive coverage of individual characteristics across a wide range of socio-demographic and cultural dimensions—including country, age, and subjective social class (Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}b). Each agent is instantiated with a natural language profile derived from real-world survey responses, and both the trustor and trustee roles are played by LLMs, conditioned on these profiles. Each profile was used once in the trustor role, and separately in the trustee role under 10 different received amounts. Two models were evaluated: Gemini 2.0 Flash (\texttt{gemini-2.0-flash-001}) and GPT‑4.1 nano.

We first analyzed how trustor decisions vary with socio-economic status. Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}c shows the distribution of send amounts stratified by subjective social class and education level. Across both models, trust behavior exhibited clear upward trends with increasing social class and education attainment. Agents identifying as upper class were substantially more likely to send higher amounts compared to those from lower classes, and similarly, individuals with advanced education levels (e.g., postgraduate) demonstrated higher trust than those with little or no formal education. These stratified patterns were evident across both models, suggesting that socio-economic background may influence trust propensity in systematic ways.

While the relative trends remain consistent, we observed differences in the overall level of trust expressed by agents simulated with different models (Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}c). In general, GPT-4.1 nano exhibited more conservative trust behavior than Gemini 2.0 Flash, with lower average send amounts observed across multiple demographic groups. This suggests that model-specific inductive biases may influence individual-level decision patterns, even when provided with identical profile information.

Trustee behavior revealed structured patterns of reciprocity that varied with the amount received (simulated by Gemini 2.0 Flash). As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}d, average return amounts increased approximately linearly with trustor send amounts, while return ratios fluctuated across transfer levels. Trustors achieved positive net profits across most transfer levels, indicating that trust was generally rewarded. This emergent pattern suggests that LLM agents simulate reciprocal norms that support mutually beneficial exchange—hallmarks of prosocial behavior in human societies. Further stratification at a fixed transfer level (Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}e) revealed that reciprocity also varied with social class, residence, and education level, suggesting that social advantage may be associated with stronger tendencies toward reciprocal behavior.

We next investigated scaling laws in LLM-powered social simulations by varying the number of agents in the trust game. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}f, the gap in send amounts between young (16–34) and older (55+) trustors becomes more pronounced as population size increases, with confidence intervals narrowing accordingly. This effect was consistent across 8 trials per population size (Fig.~\ref{fig:trust}g), indicating that trust behavior stabilizes and amplifies with scale. These results suggest that large-scale simulations can reveal latent social patterns that remain obscured in smaller populations.

\subsection{Opinion Propagation via Influencer-Influencee Interactions with One Billion Agents}\label{sec:1b}

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]{figs/1b_exp.pdf}
    \caption{\small \textbf{Opinion propagation via influencer–influencee interactions across a one-billion-agent network.} \textbf{a}, Conceptual overview of the simulation pipeline. A one-billion-agent Barabási–Albert (BA) network is constructed to emulate a realistic social topology, with influencers engaging their neighboring influencees in repeated interactions centered around a specific controversial topic. \textbf{b}, Degree distribution of the generated BA network, exhibiting a power-law tail characteristic of real-world scale-free networks, where a small fraction of nodes (influencers) possess disproportionately high connectivity. \textbf{c}, Three initial seeding regimes for influencer opinions: (1) 1D1N – equal numbers of \texttt{disagree} and \texttt{neutral}; (2) 1A1N – equal numbers of \texttt{agree} and \texttt{neutral}; and (3) Random – opinions randomly sampled from the general population. These configurations allow examination of how influencer priors shape downstream opinion dynamics. \textbf{d}, Evolution of influencee opinion distributions over simulation rounds under each seeding regime, showing distinct opinion trajectories. \textbf{e}, Opinion shift across rounds, highlighting the directional effects of influencer opinion skew. \textbf{f}, Cumulative LLM token usage over simulation size, comparing full-LLM and surrogate-assisted inference modes. The surrogate model dramatically reduces token consumption, enabling efficient large-scale simulation while preserving behavioral fidelity. \textbf{g}, Influence success rates by influencer education level and resistance to influence by influencee education level, showing asymmetric persuasion dynamics. \textbf{h}, Joint effect of education and income on influence success rate; higher socioeconomic status leads to greater influence efficacy. \textbf{i}, Validation of surrogate model fidelity in a 10,000-agent network.}
    \label{fig:1b}
\end{figure}

Opinion dynamics explores how individual attitudes evolve through social interactions, shaping collective beliefs across populations~\cite{xia2011opinion,das2014modeling}. Understanding these dynamics is essential for interpreting societal phenomena such as political polarization, rumor spreading, and public consensus formation. In particular, social networks with heterogeneous structures—such as scale-free networks generated via the Barabási–Albert (BA) model~\cite{barabasi1999emergence}—reflect real-world patterns of social connectivity, where a small subset of highly connected individuals, or ``influencers'', wield disproportionate impact on opinion formation~\cite{rochert2022two,mohamed2012identifying,brown2008influencer}. To investigate these dynamics at scale, we simulate opinion propagation over a one-billion-agent BA network, focusing on the interactions between influencers and their local neighborhoods (influencees) to uncover emergent patterns of influence, convergence, and fragmentation~\cite{chuang2023simulating}, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}a.

To instantiate the simulation, we generate a synthetic social network of one billion agents using the Barabási–Albert (BA) model (with each new node attaching to three existing nodes), yielding a sparse, scale-free topology that mirrors real-world degree heterogeneity (Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}b). Each agent is assigned a social identity by randomly sampling from a pool of 10,000 demographic profiles drawn from the World Values Survey (WVS) Wave 7 dataset~\cite{haerpfer2022world,christian2014world,lopez2023prioritizing,zhao2024worldvaluesbenchlargescalebenchmarkdataset}—ensuring diversity in age, gender, education, and socio-economic background. We designate the top 20\% of nodes by degree as \textit{influencers}, reflecting their centrality and potential for disproportionate influence in the network, while the remaining 80\% serve as \textit{influencees}.

At the start of the simulation, all agents are given a controversial statement—``AI automation will lead to mass unemployment''—and are assigned an initial opinion: \texttt{agree}, \texttt{disagree}, or \texttt{neutral}. Influencee opinions are initialized randomly, while influencer opinions are configured under three distinct seeding regimes: (1) equal proportions of \texttt{disagree} and \texttt{neutral} (1D1N), (2) equal proportions of \texttt{agree} and \texttt{neutral} (1A1N), and (3) fully randomized distribution (Random), as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}c. The simulation proceeds in rounds: in each round, 1\% of influencers are sampled uniformly at random, and each engages in topic-specific interactions with their immediate influencees. These interactions may result in updates to the influencee’s opinion, depending on both parties’ current views and profile attributes.

To scale simulation efficiently, we distill LLM behaviors into a lightweight surrogate model. We first generate a dataset of 100,000 interaction samples by prompting the LLM with diverse combinations of agent profiles and opinion states, capturing how influencees respond to influencers under varying demographic and attitudinal conditions. Then this synthetic dataset serves as supervised training data for a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) surrogate model tasked with predicting the influencee’s updated opinion given the interaction context. This surrogate replaces most LLM calls during simulation, significantly reducing token usage, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}f.

Simulation results reveal that the initial configuration of influencer opinions plays a critical role in shaping the evolution of population-wide attitudes. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}d--e, in the \textbf{1A1N} condition—where influencers predominantly hold an \texttt{agree} stance—the influencee population exhibits a clear shift toward agreement over successive rounds. Conversely, in the \textbf{1D1N} setting, where disagreeing influencers are more prevalent, the influencee group moves toward \texttt{disagree}. Notably, under the \textbf{Random} initialization—where influencer opinions mirror the distribution of the general population—opinion shifts are minimal and trend toward \texttt{neutral}. This condition also results in the lowest overall magnitude of change across the simulation, suggesting that alignment between influencer and population priors may attenuate directional influence.

These results yield two key insights. First, influencer opinion skew can steer large populations toward specific viewpoints, even when only a small fraction of influencers are active per round—highlighting the amplifying effect of centralized influence. Second, when influencers reflect the balanced opinions of the broader society, their interactions tend to moderate rather than polarize the population, leading to a drift toward neutrality and attenuated opinion change. This suggests that not only the presence of influencers but also their initial opinion structure critically determines the trajectory of collective belief dynamics.

To further understand heterogeneity in opinion influence, we analyze how individual attributes shape the likelihood of opinion change during influencer–influencee interactions. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}g, agents with higher education levels are consistently more successful at persuading others to adopt their stance, while also being more resistant to opinion change themselves. This asymmetric influence profile suggests that education confers both expressive power and cognitive resilience in social discourse. Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}h extends this analysis by examining the joint effects of education and income on influence success rates. Across all groups, influence efficacy increases with both dimensions, with the highest success observed among agents with postgraduate education and high income. These findings reflect well-documented sociological patterns: individuals with greater access to resources and knowledge often occupy more persuasive and authoritative positions in discourse, and their views tend to carry greater weight in shaping collective opinion. In simulated societies, as in real ones, structural advantages amplify communicative power—suggesting that opinion dynamics are not only shaped by who speaks, but also by who is heard.

To evaluate the fidelity of the surrogate model, we conducted a controlled validation experiment on a smaller network of 10,000 agents, where we systematically varied the proportion of influencer interactions simulated using the original LLM versus the surrogate model. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1b}i, we compare the opinion evolution trajectories under five substitution levels: 0\%, 25\%, 50\%, 75\%, and 100\% surrogate usage. The results demonstrate strong alignment between the full LLM simulation and the surrogate-assisted runs: the trajectory of opinion distributions—across \texttt{agree}, \texttt{disagree}, and \texttt{neutral} states—remains qualitatively consistent, with similar directionality and magnitude of change. In particular, the time series of influencee agree opinion counts show near-identical inflection points and convergence behavior, indicating that the surrogate model effectively captures the underlying decision patterns of the full LLM. This consistency suggests that the surrogate model not only replicates the high-level behavioral dynamics induced by the LLM, but also preserves the interaction-level semantics critical for downstream outcomes. As a result, Light Society can achieve high improvements in simulation efficiency without sacrificing behavioral fidelity—enabling opinion dynamics simulations at unprecedented scales.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have significantly reshaped agent-based social simulations, enabling more realistic representations of human cognitive processes and social behaviors~\cite{openai2024gpt4technicalreport,wang2024survey}. Leveraging extensive world knowledge and sophisticated natural language capabilities, LLM-driven agents can simulate complex human interactions, including context-aware decision-making and adaptive social responses~\cite{aher2023using}. These virtual societies, powered by LLM agents, have successfully exhibited emergent phenomena such as collective information diffusion and realistic economic market dynamics~\cite{li2023econagent,park2023generative}. Further background on traditional agent-based modeling and diverse applications of LLM agents can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:related-work-details}.

Despite these advancements, scaling LLM-based simulations to large populations remains computationally challenging. Most existing frameworks are limited to relatively small groups of agents (often fewer than 1,000) due to significant computational overhead~\cite{tang2024gensim,wang2024user}. Understanding emergent collective behaviors, however, often requires simulations involving larger populations, highlighting the critical need for scalable frameworks. Recent efforts have begun addressing these scalability challenges, developing architectures capable of efficiently simulating large-scale agent societies using parallel computation and modular designs~\cite{liu2024lmagent,piao2025agentsociety,yang2025oasis,mou2024individual,wang2025yulanonesimgenerationsocialsimulator}. % Combined some citations for brevity here

However, significant challenges persist, particularly in efficiently orchestrating large-scale LLM inference tasks and accurately representing complex societal dynamics at planetary scales~\cite{zhang2025socioverse}. Our proposed framework, Light Society, contributes directly to this area by formalizing social processes into structured state transitions governed by modular LLM-powered simulation operations, executed efficiently through event-driven mechanisms.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:con}

We introduce Light Society, an efficient framework for simulating cognitively rich societies with over one billion LLM-powered agents. By combining modular simulation operators with system-level optimizations, Light Society enables efficient, high-fidelity modeling of social behaviors at unprecedented scale.

Through large-scale trust and opinion propagation experiments, we demonstrate its ability to capture both micro-level individual variation and macro-level emergent patterns. These results position Light Society as a powerful tool for studying societal dynamics, hypothesis testing, and counterfactual analysis.

Future work will explore richer memory and learning mechanisms, longitudinal behavior modeling, and integration with real-world data to enable policy-relevant simulations and broader applications across the human sciences.

