After observing that the exit-code-based method achieves high recall but less-than-perfect precision, we further investigate the cause of the false positives (FP) by manually reviewing all FP cases. Our analysis reveals that these FP instances are actually a special subclass of fail2pass, where the test suite cannot be executed before the patch is applied but becomes runnable after patching. We refer to this phenomenon as error2pass. Figure~\ref{fig:case_study_error2pass} from the python-attrs\_\_attrs-830 case study illustrates this phenomenon. Before the patch is applied (left panel), an ImportError causes the test framework to crash during its collection phase, meaning no tests were actually executed. After applying the gold patch (right panel), this structural error is resolved, allowing all 21 tests to run and pass successfully.
% This distinction is important for benchmark construction, as including such cases can misrepresent the true capabilities of automated issue resolution methods. We recommend that future benchmark designs explicitly identify and filter out error2pass instances to ensure data quality.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/rq3_example_error2pass.pdf}
    \caption{Case study of error2pass phenomenon:  python-attrs\_\_attrs-830.}
    \label{fig:case_study_error2pass}
\end{figure}


The error2pass phenomenon reveals a fundamental flaw when such cases are used in a benchmark for issue resolution. The issue lies in the tight coupling between the solution code and the test code, as illustrated in the ``python-attrs\_\_attrs-830'' example in Figure~\ref{fig:case_study}. Here, the gold patch introduces a new function named ``to\_bool'', and the test is simultaneously updated to import and rely on that exact function name. This creates an unfair evaluation scenario. A model, which is not provided with the test file, might generate a logically perfect solution but name the function slightly differently—for instance, ``to\_boolean''. Although this patch correctly resolves the issue, it would be marked as a failure by the benchmark due to an ``ImportError'' in the test. Consequently, ``error2pass'' cases do not accurately measure a model's issue resolution ability but rather its ability to guess a specific, hidden implementation detail. To avoid underestimating model performance, it is essential to distinguish these cases from true ``fail2pass'' instances and exclude them when constructing the dataset.

