\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Datasets for GitHub Issue Resolution}

Recently, many benchmarks have been established to evaluate the ability of large language models (LLMs) to resolve real-world GitHub issues. Among them, SWE-bench~\cite{jimenez2023swe} is the most widely used, providing 2,294 Python issues with paired post-PR test suites for automated evaluation. SWE-bench Verified~\cite{openai2024swe} further improves reliability by manually validating 500 instances to ensure clarity and solvability. For multilingual and multimodal evaluation, OmniGIRL~\cite{guo2025omnigirl} offers 959 issues spanning Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, and Java, supporting both multimodal and multilingual assessment. SWE-bench Multimodal~\cite{yang2024swe} similarly contributes tasks containing code snippets and screenshots to test models’ ability to integrate visual and textual information. In addition, a range of other benchmarks and datasets have been proposed to cover diverse programming languages and evaluation needs~\cite{zan2025multi, zan2024swe, aleithan2024swe}. Besides, to improve the ability of models to resolve GitHub issues, researchers have introduced a number of training environments that support large-scale training and automated evaluation. For example, SWE-Gym~\cite{pan2024training} provides 2,438 Python tasks with Docker-based environments and unit tests for agent training. R2E-Gym~\cite{jain2025r2e} procedurally generates over 8,700 executable environments using automated test synthesis and back-translation. SWE-smith~\cite{yang2025swe} automates the construction of Python execution environments and synthesizes over 50,000 training tasks from 128 repositories, supporting agent training of models.


