The above discussion shows that the LZSE scheme is at least as powerful as grammar compression.  
A natural question is whether they are asymptotically equivalent or strictly stronger.
To address this, we present the following theorem.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:LZSE_grammar_differ}
For an arbitrarily large $n$, there is a string $T$ with $|T| \in \Theta(n)$ such that $g \in \Omega(\zseg \alpha(\zseg))$, where $\alpha(a)$ denotes the functional inverse of Ackermann's function.
\end{theorem}

To prove this, we consider the \emph{off-line range-sum problem (ORSP)}.
\begin{definition}[\cite{DBLP:journals/ijcga/ChazelleR91}]
The \emph{off-line range-sum problem (ORSP)} is defined as follows:

\noindent
{\bf Input:} a sequence $(a_1, \dots, a_m) \in X^m$, and a sequence of pairs of integers $(l_1, r_1), \dots, (l_m, r_m)$ such that $1 \leq l_i \leq r_i \leq m$ for all $i$.\\
{\bf Output:} a sequence $(q_1, \dots, q_m) \in X^m$ such that $q_i = \bigotimes_{j=l_i}^{r_i} a_j$ for each $i$.

Here, $X$ is a set and $\otimes$ is a binary operation such that $(X, \otimes)$ forms a semigroup.
\end{definition}
The following lower bound is known for this problem:
\begin{theorem}[\cite{DBLP:journals/ijcga/ChazelleR91}] \label{lem:ORSP_lowerbound}
For an arbitrarily large $m$, there exists an input to the ORSP that requires $\Omega(m \alpha(m))$ semigroup operations.
\end{theorem}
We construct an input string $T$ for the ORSP over the alphabet $\Sigma = \{ x_1, \dots, x_m, \$_1, \dots, \$_{m+1} \}$ as follows:
\[
    T = x_1 \cdots x_m \$_1 Q_1 \$_2 Q_2 \$_3 \cdots \$_m Q_m \$_{m+1}
    \text{, where } Q_i = x_{l_i} \cdots x_{r_i}.
\]
We refer to each character $\$_i$ as a \emph{delimiter}, and let $\Sigma_x = \{x_1, \dots, x_m\}$ be the set of non-delimiter characters.  
The greedy LZSE factorization of $T$ consists of the first $m$ characters and each delimiter as char factors, and each substring $x_{l_i} \cdots x_{r_i}$ as a copy factor referring to $[l_i, r_i]$.  
Thus, $\zseg \in \Theta(m)$ holds.
We now show the following lemma:
\begin{lemma} \label{lem:grammar_ORSP}
If there exists a CFG $\calG$ of size $g'$ producing $T$, the answer of the ORSP can be computed by $O(g')$ semigroup operations.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We first convert the input CFG $\calG$ into an equivalent SLP $\calG'$ of size $O(g')$.  
Since $T$ contains $2m+1$ distinct characters, we have $g' \in \Omega(m)$.
For any string $C$ over $\Sigma$, define $\head{C}$ and $\tail{C}$ as the longest prefix and suffix consisting of non-delimiter characters.  
For $C \in \Sigma_x^*$, let $f(C) = \bigotimes_{j=1}^{|C|} x_j$.  
We compute $f(\head{\exp{X}})$ and $f(\tail{\exp{X}})$ for each nonterminal $X$ in a bottom-up fashion.  
For rules $X \rightarrow x_i$, the values are trivially $x_i$.  
For a nonterminal $X$ with a rule $X \rightarrow YZ$, the values are computed as follows:
\begin{align*}
    f(\head{\exp{X}}) &=
    \begin{cases}
        f(\head{\exp{Y}}) & \text{if } \exp{Y} \text{ contains a delimiter}, \\
        f(\exp{Y}) \otimes f(\head{\exp{Z}}) & \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}\\
    f(\tail{\exp{X}}) &=
    \begin{cases}
        f(\tail{\exp{Z}}) & \text{if } \exp{Z} \text{ contains a delimiter}, \\
        f(\tail{\exp{Y}}) \otimes f(\exp{Z}) & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
An example of computing $f(\head{\exp{X}})$ and $f(\tail{\exp{X}})$ using the rules above is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:grammar_merge}.
Since the total number of nonterminals is $O(g')$, we can compute $f(\head{\exp{X}})$ and $f(\tail{\exp{X}})$ for all nonterminals using $O(g')$ semigroup operations.

Since $T$ contains delimiters $\$_1, \dots, \$_{m+1}$ in order, for each $1 \leq i \leq m$, there exists a unique rule $X \rightarrow YZ$ such that $Y$ contains $\$_i$ and $Z$ contains $\$_{i+1}$.
For such a pair $(X, Y)$, the value $q_i$ of the $i$-th range-sum query can be computed as
\[
q_i = f(Q_i) = f(\tail{\exp{Y}}) \otimes f(\head{\exp{Z}}).
\]
After computing $f(\head{\exp{X}})$ and $f(\tail{\exp{X}})$ for all $X$, all $q_i$ values can be computed in $O(m)$ time.
Thus, the total number of required operations is $O(g' + m) = O(g')$.
\end{proof}
We are now ready to prove Theorem \ref{thm:LZSE_grammar_differ}.
\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:LZSE_grammar_differ}]
Let $m$ be an arbitrarily large integer, and consider an ORSP instance requiring $\Omega(m \alpha(m))$ semigroup operations by Lemma~\ref{lem:ORSP_lowerbound}.  
Construct the string $T$ from this instance as described earlier.  
The greedy LZSE factorization of $T$ has size $\zseg \in \Theta(m)$.
Assume, for contradiction, that $g \in o(\zseg \alpha(\zseg))$ for such a string $T$.  
Then, by Lemma~\ref{lem:grammar_ORSP}, the ORSP instance could be solved in $O(g)$ operations,  
contradicting the lower bound $\Omega(m \alpha(m)) = \Omega(\zseg \alpha(\zseg))$.  
Therefore, $g \in \Omega(\zseg \alpha(\zseg))$ must hold.
\end{proof}

The same argument applies to the greedy LZ-End factorization~\cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/KreftN13}, thus it answers an open question of whether there exists a family of strings for which $\ze \in o(g)$, where $\ze$ denotes the size of the greedy LZ-End factorization, because $\ze \leq \zseg$ always holds.

We also note that the lower bound applies to run-length grammars~\cite{DBLP:conf/mfcs/NishimotoIIBT16}, an extension of CFGs that include run-length rules of the form $X \rightarrow Y^k$.  
Since the string $T$ constructed from the ORSP input contains no repeated substrings, the size of the smallest run-length grammar is the same as that of the standard grammar.  
Thus, for such $T$, we have $\grl \in \Omega(\zse \alpha(\zse))$.

Conversely, there exists a string for which the smallest run-length grammar is larger than the corresponding LZSE factorization.  
For example, the string $T = \mathtt{a}^n$ has $\grl = O(1)$ and $\zse = O(\log n)$,  
showing that $\zse \in \Omega(\grl \log n)$ for this case.  
This bound is tight, as $g \in O(\grl \log n)$ and $g \leq \zse$ together imply $\zse \in O(\grl \log n)$.


