Table~\ref{tab:compression_times} shows the computation times for each factorization or grammar. 
As expected, \textsc{lzss} and \textsc{lz77} are the fastest due to their simple strategies.  
While \textsc{lzse} is slightly slower than them, it still runs significantly faster than \textsc{lzend} and \textsc{repair} across all datasets.


Unlike LZ77 and LZSS, which represent each source position as an offset in the string, LZ-End and LZSE can use factor indices, and LZSE further can encode reference lengths as the number of referenced factors.  
This leads to more compact representations per factor, and the same encoding can be applied to grammar-based compressors like Re-Pair by converting the grammar to the corresponding LZSE factorization.
Furthermore, in LZSE, most copy factors are expected to refer to only a small number of consecutive factors.
It allows further compression using zeroth-order entropy compression.
We evaluate this by computing the zeroth-order empirical entropy of each field in the factor representations.

Table~\ref{tab:encoded_size_proteins} presents the zeroth-order empirical entropy for each symbol group in the \texttt{proteins} dataset (see also Table~\ref{tab:all_dataset_entropy_comparison} in Appendix~\ref{app:other_datasets} for other datasets).
We applied LZSE-style encoding to Re-Pair grammar, denoted as \textsc{repair-se}.
We observe that \textsc{lzse} and \textsc{repair-se} exhibit notably smaller entropy values for the \textsf{source} component compared to \textsc{lz77} and \textsc{lzss}.
Although \textsc{lzend} also achieves a low entropy for the \textsf{source} field, this comes at the cost of a significantly higher entropy in the \textsf{length} field.
In contrast, both \textsc{lzse} and \textsc{repair-se} not only reduce the entropy of \textsf{source},  
but also exhibit lower entropy in the \textsf{length} component compared to \textsc{lz77} and \textsc{lzss}.  
Notably, the \textsf{length} entropy in \textsc{repair-se} falls below 1 bit, suggesting strong synergy between Re-Pair's structure and LZSE-style encoding.


