\begin{theorem} \label{thm:SymCPD_lineartime}
Given an LZSE factorization $\calF = (F_1, \dots, F_\zse)$, all heavy paths in the derivation DAG $\calD$ can be computed in $O(\zse)$ time.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The original symmetric centroid path decomposition selects an edge $(u, v)$ as a heavy edge if it satisfies the conditions $\lfloor \lg \ns{u} \rfloor = \lfloor \lg \ns{v} \rfloor$ and $\lfloor \lg \ne{u} \rfloor = \lfloor \lg \ne{v} \rfloor$, where $\ns{v}$ and $\ne{v}$ denote the number of maximal paths starting at and ending at node $v$, respectively.  
For any node $u$, the heavy edge among its outgoing edges points to the child with the largest value of $\ns{v}$.

Let $s_1, \dots, s_{\zse}$ and $e_1, \dots, e_{\zse}$ be arrays representing $\ns{F_i}$ and $\ne{F_i}$ for each factor $F_i \in \calF$.  
We initialize all entries to $0$, and compute the values as follows:
To compute $s_i$ (number of paths starting from $F_i$), process $i = 1, \dots, \zse$ in increasing order:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $F_i$ is a char factor, set $s_i \gets 1$.
    \item If $F_i$ is a copy factor referring to $F_l, \dots, F_r$, set $s_i \gets \sum_{j=l}^r s_j$.
\end{itemize}
To compute $e_i$ (number of paths ending at $F_i$), process $i = \zse, \dots, 1$ in decreasing order:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $e_i = 0$, set $e_i \gets 1$.
    \item If $F_i$ is a copy factor referring to $F_l, \dots, F_r$, update $e_j \gets e_j + e_i$ for all $j = l$ to $r$.
\end{itemize}
Both procedures can be implemented in $O(\zse)$ time using a prefix-sum technique.

Once the values of $s_i$ and $e_i$ are computed, we determine, for each copy factor $F_i$ referring to $F_l, \dots, F_r$, the index $j \in [l, r]$ that maximizes $s_j$.
This corresponds to solving a range maximum query on each interval $[l, r]$.  
All such queries can be answered in total $O(\zse)$ time by reducing them to offline lowest common ancestor (LCA) queries on a Cartesian tree~\cite{DBLP:journals/cacm/Vuillemin80} and applying a linear-time offline LCA algorithm~\cite{DBLP:conf/focs/Harel80}.
For each resulting candidate edge from $F_j$ to $F_i$, we then check whether it satisfies the heavy edge condition.
Since the number of such candidate edges is $O(\zse)$, this step also runs in linear time.  
Thus, all heavy edges in the derivation DAG can be computed in $O(\zse)$ time.  
Once all heavy edges are identified, the corresponding heavy paths can be constructed straightforwardly.
\end{proof}

