At the intersection between traditional CPU architectures and more specialized options such as FPGAs or ASICs lies the family of reconfigurable hardware architectures, termed Coarse-Grained Reconfigurable Arrays (CGRAs). CGRAs are composed of a 2-D array of processing elements (PE), tightly integrated with each other, each capable of performing arithmetic and logic operations.
