\section{Forward-Pass FLOP Analysis of Token Mixers}
\label{sec:flop_analysis_token_mixers}

This section derives closed‑form expressions for the floating‑point operations (FLOPs) required by the different recurrent and linear‑attention token mixers that are presented in this paper, and contrasts them with classical softmax self‑attention.  All counts are \textbf{per layer, forward pass only}, aggregated over all heads.  Projection layers, (un-)embedding, residual additions, normalisation, and other feed‑forward blocks are \emph{not} included so that every token mixer is compared on equal footing.

\paragraph{Notation and assumptions}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $L$ – causal sequence length (tokens seen by the layer).
  \item $H$ - number of heads in the model (this applies to all trained models except the HGRN-1 model where $H=1$ by design).
  \item $d$ – width of a single head. Vectors within a single head live in $\mathbb{R}^{d}$. For a model dimension $d_\text{model}$ with $H$ heads we have $d = d_\text{model}/H$.
  \item One FLOP is a single floating‑point multiply \emph{or} add.
  \item Only the token-mixer is costed.  Projections, MLPs, norms, residuals are identical across models and therefore omitted.
  \item All counts below are \emph{per token, per layer, summed over all heads}.  Multiply by $L$ for a sequence, and by the number of layers to estimate a full network.
  % \item When a model keeps an internal \emph{state}, that state is updated once per new token $t$ and then used to produce the head output $o_t$.
  % \item All results assume the most economical implementation (rank‑1 tricks, diagonal simplifications, avoiding explicit matrix–matrix forms, etc.).
  % \item To obtain the layer cost multiply the per‑token result by $L$; to obtain the model cost multiply further by $H$.
\end{itemize}

