\section{Discussion}

Figure 5 shows an example of normal COT and MCTS-SQL to solve the same user's query. Through the error analysis, we can find that the syntax and structure generated by the COT are generally correct, but due to the hallucination of LLM, it struggles with detailed information and complex constraints. The feedback and iterative optimization mechanism of MCTS helps reduce errors caused by these details, thus improving the performance of SQL generation.

\section{Conclusion}
In conclusion, this paper introduces MCTS-SQL, a novel framework that utilizes the Monte Carlo Tree Search to enhance the LLMs to address challenges in Text-to-SQL tasks. Experimental results from the Spider and BIRD demonstrate the significant advantages of our method, achieving state-of-the-art performance, especially in challenging queries. Specifically,we achieve an EX \textbf{69.40\%} on the BIRD with \textbf{51.48\%} on the challenging set, outperforming existing method by \textbf{3.41\%}. These results show the potential of MCTS-SQL for real-world database applications. However, the MCTS uses multiple iterations which inevitable increases token consumption and time.  In the future work, we will explore some methods to solve the constrain.



