\subsection{Assignment problem}

\begin{example}[Assignment problem]\label{ex:opt_assig}

The Assignment Problem (\AP) is a fundamental combinatorial optimization problem.
Its goal consists in assigning a set $i\in N$ of facilities (or tasks) to a set of locations $j\in N$ (or workers) in a one to one basis while minimizing assignment costs. 
%We assume that potential facilities and locations share the same set of locations indexed by the set $N$.  
%This can be done without loss of generality, as otherwise, one could introduce dummy nodes and set the facility opening and allocation costs accordingly.
%In graph theory, the assignment problem consists of finding, in a weighted bipartite graph, a matching of a given size, in which the sum of weights of the edges is minimum. 

This problem is equivalent to finding the permutation $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}_n$ which minimizes the total cost. Every permutation $\phi$ of the set $N$ can be represented by an $n \times n$ matrix $X=\left(x_{i j}\right)_{i,j \in N}$,  called a permutation matrix 
such that $x_{i j}$ equals 1 if $\varphi(i)=j$. This matrix can be characterized by following assignment constraints
\begin{subequations}


\label{eq:qap_cons}
\begin{align}
	 & x_i=\sum_{j=1}^n c_{ij} x_{ij} & i \in N,  \label{cons:ap_0}  \\
	 & \sum_{i=1}^n x_{i j}=1,        & j \in N, \label{cons:ap_1}   \\
	 & \sum_{j=1}^n x_{i j}=1,        & i \in N, \label{cons:ap_2}   \\
	 & x_{i j} \in\{0,1\},            & i, j\in N. \label{cons:ap_3}
\end{align}    
\end{subequations}


Constraints \eqref{cons:ap_0} and \eqref{cons:ap_3} define the cost function of the assignment problem.
Constraints (\ref{cons:ap_1}) guarantee that all facilities are assigned to exactly one location and constraints (\ref{cons:ap_2}) that every location can only get one facility assigned.

