\subsection{Generalization of location problems}

Let $N = \{1,...,n\}$ be a set of indices representing facilities 
and assume that we are given a cost matrix $C= (c_{ij})_{i,j \in N}$, where  $c_{ij} \geq 0$ represents the cost of allocating the facility placed at position $i$ to the location at $j$. 
Facility location problems are concerned with attaining the optimal placement of facilities in order to minimize costs under certain considerations. 
To extend and provide a general framework for facility location problems, the Discrete Ordered Median Location Problem (\DOMP) was introduced by \cite{Nickel2006}, which could be used to model different locations problems, as the $p$-median, $p$-center or centdian problems, but since negative and non-monotonic weight vectors are allowed, problems of obnoxious location, preference modeling or other abstract concepts such as, for example, fairness can also be considered. 

In order to model the problem, assignment varibales $x_{ij}$ are now equal to 1 if a facility placed at position $i$ is allocated to site at $j$, and zero otherwise. 
Then, the $p$-median polytope can be described by means of the following set of constraints:


\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:domp_cons}
\begin{align}
& \sum_{j \in N} x_{jj}=p, &            \label{cons:domp_1} \\
& \sum_{j \in N} x_{ij}=1, & i \in N,   \label{cons:domp_2} \\
& x_{ij} \leq x_{jj},      & i,j \in N. \label{cons:domp_3} 
% x_{i j} \in\{0,1\}, \quad i, j=1, \ldots, n .
\end{align}    
\end{subequations}


The group of constraints (\ref{cons:domp_1}), (\ref{cons:domp_2}) and (\ref{cons:domp_3}) models the allocation of clients to facilities. Specifically, constraint (\ref{cons:domp_1}) fixes the number of facilities in the network to $p$, constraints (\ref{cons:domp_2}) ensure that each client is allocated to exactly one facility and constraints (\ref{cons:domp_3}) ensure that no client is allocated to a non-facility node.
In the following, we will refer to the integer lattice points given by (\ref{eq:domp_cons}) as $\mathcal{X}_{L}$.
Therefore, given $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^n$ and $\M \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, an ordered generalization to assignment problems is obtained by considering the following problem:
\vspace{-0.75cm}


\begin{subequations}
\label{form:Q_assingment}
\begin{align}
\min & \ \x^t C \M C^t \x, & \label{cons:Q_a_0}  \\
& x \in \mathcal{X}_L,                     & \label{cons:Q_a_1}  \\
& x \in \mathcal{O}.                       & \label{cons:Q_a_2} 
\end{align}
\end{subequations}


\vspace{-1cm}
For this problem we can propose a better linearization follwoing Kaufmann and Broeckx linearization strategy, see \cite{Kaufman1978}, obtained by applying the general linearization strategy proposed by \cite{Glover1975}, which reduces the number of variables to consider.

