\subsection{Weighted regression with correlated data}

Given a response variable $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$, model matrix $\mathbf{X}_{n \times p}$ (with first column of ones), regression coefficients $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ and error $\varepsilon \in \mathbb{R}^n$, consider the following multiple linear regression model with 

$$y = \mathbf{X} \beta+\varepsilon.$$

Given data for the $i$-th sample $\left(y_i, \mathbf{x}_i\right), i \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$, the $i$-th residual is denoted by 

$$r_i(\beta) = y_i-\mathbf{x}_i^{\top} \beta \text{ for } i \in \{1, \ldots,n\}$$ 

The traditional Least Squares $(\mathrm{LS})$ estimator is given by

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:betaLS}
\widehat{\beta}^{(\mathrm{LS})} := \arg \min_{\beta} \sum_{i=1}^n r_i^2,
\end{equation}

Problems with the LS estimator

\begin{itemize}
    \item Hypothesis:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Normally distributed residuals
        \item Centered in 0 (unbiased)
        \item Equal variance $\sigma^2$ (homocedasticity)
    \end{itemize}
    \item Additional problems:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Correlated data
        \item Highly sensitive to outliers in the response variable, i.e., vertical deviations.
        \item Highly sensitive to outliers in the covariate space (explanatory variables), i.e., horizontal deviations.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

