\begin{abstract}
	In this study, we present a simple and intuitive method for accelerating optimal Reeds-Shepp path computation. Our approach uses geometrical reasoning to analyze the behavior of optimal paths, resulting in a new partitioning of the state space and a further reduction in the minimal set of viable paths. We revisit and reimplement classic methodologies from the literature, which lack contemporary open-source implementations, to serve as benchmarks for evaluating our method. Additionally, we address the under-specified Reeds-Shepp planning problem where the final orientation is unspecified.
	We perform exhaustive experiments to validate our solutions. Compared to the modern C++ implementation of the original Reeds-Shepp solution in the Open Motion Planning Library, our method demonstrates a $15\times$ speedup, while classic methods achieve a $5.79\times$ speedup. Both approaches exhibit machine-precision differences in path lengths compared to the original solution. We release our proposed C++ implementations for both the accelerated and under-specified Reeds-Shepp problems as open-source code.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Nonholonomic Motion Planning, Constrained Motion Planning, Motion and Path Planning, Computational Geometry
\end{IEEEkeywords}
