    /* Safety annotation to be provided by developers: ? */
    pub unsafe fn set_len(&mut self, l:usize) {
        self.len = l;
    }
    pub fn get(&self, x: usize) -> u8 {
        if x < self.len {
            /* Safety annotation from Rustdoc for offset(): 
                pub const unsafe fn offset(self, count: isize) -> *const T
                1) The offset in bytes, count * size_of::<T>(), computed on mathematical integers (without wrapping around), must 
                fit in an isize. 
                2) If the computed offset is non-zero, then self must be derived from a pointer to some allocated object, 
                and the entire memory range between self and the result must be in bounds of that allocated object. 
            */
            unsafe { *self.ptr.offset(x as isize) }
        } else {
            0
        }
    }
}
fn f1(v: Vec<u8>) {
    let (p,l,_c) = v.into_raw_parts();
    let s1 = St1::from(p, l); 
    f2(); 
}
fn f2() {
    let mut v = vec![1,2,3];
    let (p,l,_c) = v.into_raw_parts();
    let s2 = unsafe { St2::from(p, 0)};
    unsafe { s2.set_len(p, l)};
    let t2 = s2.get(0);
}
\end{lstlisting}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Unsafety Perils}\label{sec:perils}
The approach of unsafe code encapsulation is not only ideal but also effective in practice~\cite{evans2020rust}. However, as the ecosystem continues to expand, the limitations of the design gradually emerge and may impede the future development of the language. Specifically, the soundness check of interior unsafe code primarily relies on developers. While this task may be manageable for small-scale projects with only a few lines of unsafe code, it becomes challenging in real-world Rust projects as the number of unsafe code snippets grows. Furthermore, third-party library developers often prioritize feature implementation over rigorous soundness verification, and they may lack the expertise of standard library contributors, leading to latent unsoundness issues. Real-world Rust projects often involve complex structs and traits, requiring developers to consider all combinations of member functions to ensure soundness. Next, we employ Listing \ref{lst:case} to demonstrate the challenge of unsafe code handling.

Firstly, there is no actionable guideline on whether a function should be declared as safe or not. The safety declarations of different methods within a struct are interdependent. Listing \ref{lst:case} contains two structs \texttt{St1} and \texttt{St2}, both have two fields: \texttt{ptr} and \texttt{len}. However, they employ different strategies in safety declaration. In \texttt{St1}, the constructor \texttt{from()} is safe, while the method \texttt{get()} is unsafe. In comparison, the constructor of \texttt{St2} is unsafe, while the method \texttt{get()} is safe. Both patterns are popular in real-world Rust projects. As long as they meet the safety promise of no undefined behavior without using unsafe code, they are both valid. The pattern of \texttt{St1} is much more straightforward because \texttt{from()} contains no unsafe code and cannot lead to undefined behavior by itself. While the pattern of \texttt{St2} is less common than \texttt{St1}, it has the advantage of avoiding the need to declare other methods as unsafe, especially when there are multiple methods containing interior unsafe code, similar to \texttt{get()}. Besides, both data structs contain a \texttt{set\_len()} method: one is safe, whereas the other should be declared as unsafe. 

Secondly, there is little practical guidance on how to document the safety properties of an unsafe function. The annotation of safety properties for unsafe APIs plays a crucial role in verifying the soundness of safety encapsulation. Intuitively, an API containing interior unsafe code can only be declared as safe if all the safety requirements of its internal safe APIs are met, otherwise, it should delegate the safety requirements. For example, to annotate the safety property of \texttt{get} within \texttt{St1}, developers should understand the safety property of its interior unsafe code, \textit{i.e.,} \texttt{slice::from\_raw\_parts()} from the Rust standard library. The Rustdoc of \texttt{std} provides four explicit safety requirements in textual form to guide developers in using the API safely~\cite{slice-doc}. Developers may directly employ the same safety properties of \texttt{slice::from\_raw\_parts()} for \texttt{get()}. Yet, the annotation can be further detailed according to the context of usage, \textit{e.g.,} replacing the generic type of the return value \texttt{\&[T]} to the monomorphized type \texttt{\&[u8]}. 

However, the issue becomes complicated when considering the semantics of the method and the interdependency of methods within a struct. Take \texttt{St2} as an example: the method \texttt{get()} has two safety properties, but the length check alone cannot guarantee that these conditions are satisfied. Specifically, it cannot ensure that \texttt{self.ptr} points to an allocated object. This is why the constructor \texttt{from()} is declared as unsafe, signaling to developers that there are safety requirements to be followed when using the API. It is possible to trigger undefined behaviors with \texttt{get()}, but only after the object has been constructed via unsafe \texttt{from()}. In this way, the safety promises of Rust can be upheld. However, \texttt{St2} is unsound because the object can also be constructed using the literal syntax, since both fields of \texttt{St2} are public. As a result, it is possible to trigger undefined behaviors without using the unsafe marker. The bug can be fixed by making at least one field of \texttt{St2} private, thereby disabling the literal constructor.

\section{Unsafe Code Annotation}\label{sec:annotation}
While existing textual documentation of unsafe code is readable, it is often ad hoc and sometimes ambiguous, making it unsuitable for formal verification or soundness audits. In this section, we propose a novel domain-specific language for describing the safety properties of unsafe code. 

\subsection{Safety Property Annotation Language}
The safety property annotation language is a context-free language defined as follows. 

\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\texttt{SP } &\mapsto \texttt{ \textquotesingle!\textquotesingle? Tag \textquotesingle(\textquotesingle\ Input, (, Input)}^*\texttt{ \textquotesingle)\textquotesingle} \\
\texttt{Tag } &\mapsto \texttt{ [a-zA-Z]}^+ \\
\texttt{Input } &\mapsto \texttt{ (FnParID | RetVal | TypePar | AriExpr | ValRange | AddrRange | Spec) } \\
\texttt{AriExpr } &\mapsto \texttt{  BinOp\ \textquotesingle(\textquotesingle\ AriExpr, AriExpr \textquotesingle)\textquotesingle\ |  UniOp\ \textquotesingle(\textquotesingle\ AriExpr \textquotesingle)\textquotesingle\ | FnParID | Num | Size } \\
\texttt{Size } &\mapsto \texttt{ sizeof \textquotesingle(\textquotesingle\ TypePar \textquotesingle)\textquotesingle  } \\
\texttt{ValRange } &\mapsto \texttt{ Val | (( \textquotesingle(\textquotesingle\ | \textquotesingle[\textquotesingle\ ) Val\ \textquotesingle,\textquotesingle\ Val (\ \textquotesingle]\textquotesingle\ |\ \textquotesingle)\textquotesingle\ ))} \\
\texttt{AddrRange } &\mapsto \texttt{ (p, T, len) | (s, range) | vec | ...} \\
\texttt{Spec } &\mapsto \texttt{ Num | Type | Trait | Allocator | ...} \\
\end{split}
\end{equation}

Each property is specified with a tag and multiple inputs, where the tag represents the property name, and the inputs correspond to the detailed arguments of the unsafe API being annotated. The arguments typically include the necessary parameters, type parameters, or the return value of the API. In addition, the arguments may include arithmetic expressions, value ranges, address ranges, or other specifications.

For example, the first property of \texttt{slice::from\_raw\_parts(data: *const T, len: usize) -> \&'a [T]} requires that the first parameter, \texttt{data}, must be non-null. Therefore, we can describe the property as \texttt{!Null(data)}. Additionally, it requires that \texttt{data} must be valid for reading \texttt{len * mem::sizeof::<T>()} bytes. This property requires \texttt{data} must point to an allocated memory space ranging from \texttt{data} to \texttt{data + len * mem::sizeof::<T>()}, represented as \texttt{Allocated(data, len, T, any)}. However, there are implied relationships among the two properties, \textit{i.e.}, \texttt{Allocated(data, len, T, any)} > \texttt{!Null(data)}. Therefore, we only need to use \texttt{Allocated(data, len, T, any)} for annotation. The first property also requires that \texttt{data} must be properly aligned, which can be represented as \texttt{Align(data, T)}. The second textual property requires the data must be initialized, which can be represented as \texttt{Init(data, T, len)}. As \texttt{Init(data, T, len)} implies \texttt{Allocated(data, len, T, any)}, we can also ignore the \texttt{Allocated} property. The third textual property requires the memory referenced by the slice must not be mutated during the lifetime \texttt{\textquotesingle a}. We can represent the property with \texttt{Alias(self.ptr, 0)}, where \texttt{0} is the identifier of the return value. The property indicates that this unsafe API may create a hazardous status violating the exclusive mutability principle. The fourth property requires no overflow when performing arithmetic operations, and we can represent the property as \texttt{ValidNum(add(data, mul(sizeof(T), len)), (0, isize::MAX])}.

Next, we introduce the detailed design of our safety properties.

