\subsubsection{Layout}
According to the Rust reference book~\cite{typelayout}, there are three key attributes related to layout.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Alignment}: 
    Each type has an alignment requirement of $2^x$ bytes, where $x \geq 0$. The starting address \texttt{p} of an object of type \texttt{T} is considered aligned if \texttt{p \% alignment(T) = 0}. Additionally, the size of type \texttt{T} should always be a multiple of its alignment, \textit{i.e.}, \texttt{sizeof(T) \% alignment(T) = 0}. These requirements can be captured by the alignment property \texttt{Align(p, T)}. 
    \item \textit{Size}: If the size of a type can be determined during compiling time, the object is sized. We annotate the property as \texttt{Sized(T, any)}. Otherwise, the type is dynamically sized, annotated as \texttt{Sized(T, unknown)}. Besides, an unsafe API may require the size of an argument object to be non-ZST (zero-sized type), such as the \texttt{NonNull::offset\_from(self, origin: NonNull<T>) -> isize} method. This requirement can be expressed with the property \texttt{Size(T, !0)}, or \texttt{!ZST} as a variant or shorthand.
    \item \textit{Padding}: Padding refers to unused space between successive elements of the same type, ensuring proper alignment. It is included when calculating the size of the type. A safety property may require that type \texttt{T} has no padding, represented as \texttt{!Padding(T)}. For example, \texttt{raw\_eq()} requires that the objects being compared contain no padding, because the padding contents may affect the comparison result.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Pointer}
This type concerns pointer validity. For example, some APIs (\textit{e.g.,} \texttt{NonNull::new\_unchecked(ptr: *mut T) -> NonNull<T>}) require the parameter pointer to be non-null, which is represented by \texttt{!Null(p)}. Additionally, some unsafe APIs (\textit{e.g.,} \texttt{pointer::offset(self, count: isize) -> *mut T}) require the pointer to be non-dangling, meaning it must point to a valid memory location, either within allocated memory or the stack. We represent the property as \texttt{Allocated(p, T, len, A)}, which specifies that the memory space of size \texttt{sizeof(T) * len} pointed to by the pointer \texttt{p} must be allocated by the allocator \texttt{A}. The property \texttt{InBound(p, T, len, arange)} ensures that the memory space is not only allocated but allocated for the same object. Finally, the property \texttt{!Overlap(dst, src, len, arange)} ensures that the two memory spaces pointed to by \texttt{dst} and \texttt{src} do not overlap. 

\subsubsection{Content}
This category ensures that the contents of allocated memory must be valid or adhere to the type requirements. For instance, the generic-typed API, \texttt{Box::assume\_init(self) -> Box<[T], A>} requires the memory of the generic-typed value to be properly initialized before calling the method. For other concrete types, there are specific properties:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Valid Number}: The result of integer arithmetic must be within the valid range for integer types, such as \texttt{isize}, \texttt{i32}, or \texttt{f32}. We annotate this property as \texttt{ValidNum(expr, vrange)}, where the first parameter is an arithmetic expression, and the second indicates the valid value ranges. This annotation also implies that intermediate results during the evaluation of the expression must not overflow. 
    \item \textit{Valid String}: Rust uses UTF-8 encoding for strings. Therefore, this property requires that the memory content within an address space must be valid UTF-8, annotated as \texttt{ValidString(arange)}. In practice, the address range for specific APIs is specified in different formats. For example, we can annotate \texttt{String::from\_utf8\_unchecked(bytes: Vec<u8>) -> String} with \texttt{ValidString(bytes)}.
    \item \textit{Valid CStr}: This is the C string format, which requires the last byte to be a null terminator. \texttt{CStr::from\_ptr<'a>(ptr: *const i8) -> \&'a CStr} is an example API with the requirement. 
\end{itemize}

Furthermore, objects of an enumerated type can be unwrapped directly via unsafe APIs without performing a validity check. We use the annotation \texttt{Unwrap(x, T)} to specify the conditions required for safe unwrapping. For instance, \texttt{Option::unwrap\_unchecked(self) -> T} can be annotated with \texttt{Unwrap(self, Some(T))}, and \texttt{Result::unwrap\_unchecked(self) -> T} with \texttt{Unwrap(self, Ok(T))}. Finally, \texttt{Typed(p, T)} means the memory pointed by the pointer can be used for creating an object of type T. Rust has such safety requirements for several constructors named \texttt{from\_raw}, such as \texttt{Rc::from\_raw(ptr: *const T) -> Rc<T>} which requires the pointers must be returned from an \texttt{Rc} object via the \texttt{Rc::into\_raw(this: Rc<T, A>) -> *const T} method.

\subsubsection{Alias}
This category relates to the context of ownership, borrowing, and lifetime rules enforced by the Rust compiler. These mechanisms are fundamental to achieving exclusive mutability in Rust. The property \texttt{Owning(p)} means that the object pointed to by \texttt{p} is going to be owned, and it should not have any other owners prior to that. \texttt{Box::from\_raw(raw: *mut T)-> Box<T>} is an API that turns the raw pointer into an owned value and should be annotated with \texttt{Owning(raw)}. Note that \texttt{Owning} is generally considered as a precondition, as a value is not allowed to have two owners even temporarily due to the risk of double-free vulnerabilities during unwinding~\cite{xu2021memory,li2021mirchecker}. The property \texttt{Alias(p1, p2)} means that two parameters point to the same memory space and may violate the requirement for exclusive mutability. This can occur, for example, when converting a raw pointer into a reference using methods like \texttt{pointer::as\_mut<'a>(self) -> Option<\&'a mut T>} or \texttt{pointer::as\_ref<'a>(self) -> Option<\&'a T>}. Different from \texttt{Owning}, temporary violations of alias rules can be useful in certain scenarios (\textit{e.g.,} double-linked list) and are therefore permitted as long as the program remains safe. As a result, we classify the safety property of \texttt{Alias} as a hazard. We will elaborate more on the classifications in Section~\ref{sec:spusage}. Finally, we use \texttt{Alive(p, l)} to represent that the lifetime of the object pointed to by \texttt{p} must be longer than the lifetime \texttt{l}.

\subsubsection{Miscellaneous}
There are specific safety properties that apply to particular contexts. For instance, pinning is a characteristic that ensures an object remains at the same memory address after being created and cannot be moved. Rust provides this feature via the \texttt{Pin} struct, and we annotate such requirement for methods within this struct as \texttt{Pinned(p)}. Another property related to memory access is volatile, which indicates that the memory may be subject to concurrent access and may change. In such cases, compilers should reload the value from memory rather than using the previous copy. Read or write operations via pointers (\textit{e.g.}, \texttt{ptr::read<T>(src: *const T) -> T}) generally require the memory to be non-volatile. Otherwise, developers should use volatile methods. We represent this requirement with \texttt{!Volatile(p)}. 
For file read and write operations, methods typically require that the file must be open. We annotate this property as \texttt{Opened(fd)}, such as for the method \texttt{UdpSocket::from\_raw\_socket(sock: RawSocket) -> UdpSocket}.
Additionally, we use \texttt{Trait(T, trait)} to specify the condition that a type T implements a particular trait. Finally, \texttt{!Reachable()} is a special requirement indicating the current statement should not be reached during execution. The function \texttt{intrinsics::unreachable() -> !} requires the safety property.

\subsubsection{Compound Safety Properties}
By combining primitive safety properties, we define compound safety properties to express complex constraints commonly found in the Rust standard library documentation. These compound properties capture recurring patterns of complicated safety requirements, enabling more concise and reusable annotations.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{Deref(p, T, len)}: This property requires that dereferencing a pointer \texttt{p} of type \texttt{T} over a range from \texttt{p} to \texttt{p+Sizeof(T)*len} is safe. It consists of two primitive safety properties: \texttt{Allocated(p, T, len, any)} and \texttt{InBound(p, T, len)}.

    \item \texttt{ValidPtr(p, T, len)}: Rustdoc has specified several criteria for a valid pointer~\cite{moduleptr}. The criteria distinguish zero-sized types (ZSTs) from others. For memory accesses of ZSTs, every pointer is valid. But for non-ZSTs, validity requires the memory to be dereferenceable. We can therefore formulate the compound safety property as \texttt{Size(T, 0) || (Size(T, !0) \&\& \texttt{Deref(p, T, len))}}

    \item \texttt{Ptr2Ref(p, T)}: This property specifies the safety requirements when converting raw pointers to references. According to Rustdoc, it does not differentiate whether \texttt{T} is ZST or not. The safety property can be represented as \texttt{Align(p, T) \&\& Deref(p, T, 1) \&\& Alias(p, 0)}.

    \item \texttt{Layout(p, layout)}: Memory allocation APIs (\textit{e.g.}, \texttt{Global::alloc(\&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8}) may impose constraints on memory layouts. These constraints typically require that a raw pointer be aligned to the alignment specified by the layout, and that the memory it points to be allocated on the heap with the size specified in the layout. The property can be expressed as \texttt{ValidNum(rem(p, layout.align), 0) \&\& Allocated(p, u8, layout.size, heap)}.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Unsupported Safety Properties}\label{unsupported SP}

There are two types of safety properties that we currently do not support.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Unsafe trait}: Implementing an unsafe trait (\textit{e.g.,} \texttt{Send}, \texttt{Sync} and \texttt{GlobalAlloc}) is itself an unsafe operation, as it requires satisfying certain safety guarantees. However, these requirements are highly specific to each data type, making it difficult to represent the associated safety properties in a general or explicit form.

    \item \textit{Non-language-level UBs}: Our annotation language does not handle non-language-level undefined behaviors well, particularly those related to the system environment (\textit{e.g.,} \texttt{env::set\_var()}) or the correctness of specific functions (\textit{e.g.,} \texttt{CursorMut::insert\_after\_unchecked()}). The properties required to ensure safety in these cases are either difficult to verify or not generally applicable. Besides, there is a significant portion of intrinsic APIs are implemented at the compiler backend, which also fall outside the scope of our language-level safety property framework.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Usage of Safety Properties} \label{sec:spusage}
Safety properties can be used in three ways:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Precondition}: A safety property is used as a precondition if it must be satisfied before calling the API. Most safety properties are used as preconditions in practice.
    \item \textit{Hazardous status (hazard)}: Some safety properties describe a hazardous state that can occur after calling an unsafe API. For example, \texttt{!Init(dst, T, count)} can describe a hazardous state after calling \texttt{ptr::copy<T>(src: *const T, dst: *mut T, count: usize)} when the source memory is uninitialized.
    \item \textit{Optional precondition (option)}: Satisfying an optional precondition ensures the safety of calling an unsafe API, but it is not mandatory. For example, \texttt{Size(T, num)} ensures the safety of calling \texttt{Layout::for\_value\_raw<T>(t: *const T) -> Layout}, while \texttt{Size(T, unknown)} is also supported but with additional requirements. Similarly, \texttt{Trait(T, Unpin)} ensures the safety of calling \texttt{Pin::new\_unchecked(pointer: Ptr) -> Pin<Ptr>}, but it is not required. If \texttt{!Trait(T, Unpin)} is the case, we can use \texttt{Pinned(p)} to describe the hazardous state. 
\end{itemize}

\section{Crate-level Unsafe Code Annotation}\label{sec:method}
Previously, we discussed the annotation approach for individual unsafe APIs. In practice, however, an unsafe API may be coupled with other APIs, such as through a caller and callee relationship or through co-location within the same struct. As discussed in Section \ref{sec:perils}, such coupling plays a critical role in determining function safety declarations and safety property annotations. To the best of our knowledge, there is currently no clear guidance on this matter. This section presents a novel method for analyzing the safety properties that need to be declared by APIs, based on how unsafe code propagates.

\subsection{Problem Analysis}
Given a Rust crate, there are three typical scenarios to be considered for unsafe code handling: 
\begin{itemize} 
\item \textit{Original unsafe code annotation}: We refer to unsafe code in a Rust crate as \textit{original unsafe code} (or \textit{root unsafe code}) if it is introduced without any internal unsafe code, or if it is an unsafe API originating from another library outside the crate, such as the standard library or third-party libraries. For such unsafe code, the safety requirements must be explicitly documented by the code designer or library provider.

\item \textit{Safe property delegation}: If an unsafe API contains internal unsafe code, developers must ensure that the associated safety properties are properly delegated to the higher-level unsafe API.

\item \textit{Safety encapsulation}: If an API contains internal unsafe code but fully enforces all necessary safety properties, it can be safely exposed as a safe API.
\end{itemize}

The strategies for handling unsafe code outlined above have already become a de facto consensus within the official Rust community and among its developers. However, clear guidelines for annotating unsafe code and verifying the consistency of these annotations in real-world Rust projects are still lacking. For instance, the unsafe API, \texttt{St2::from()} in Listing~\ref{lst:case}, does not contain any internal unsafe code, yet it still requires safety property annotations, which are not immediately apparent. In such cases, we must consider the enforced execution order of the constructor and other member methods of the object.

\subsection{Unsafety Propagation Graph}
We now introduce the \textit{unsafety propagation graph}, a tool that tracks the influence of unsafe code by considering both static functions (which can be called directly) and dynamic methods (which must be invoked through an object pointer).

\begin{definition}[Unsafety Propagation Graph (UPG)]
A UPG $\mathcal{G(F,M,C,E)}$ is a directed graph that tracks the propagation of unsafe code through function calls and object flows in a Rust crate.

where
\begin{itemize}
    %\item $\mathcal{U}$ is a set of nodes for unsafe code snippets.
    \item $\mathcal{F}$ is the set of nodes representing functions and static methods, including both safe ones $\mathcal{F}_s$ and unsafe ones $\mathcal{F}_u$.

    \item $\mathcal{M}$ is the set of nodes representing dynamic methods, including both safe ones $\mathcal{M}_s$ and unsafe ones $\mathcal{M}_u$.

    \item $\mathcal{C}$ is the set of nodes representing constructors, including both safe ones $\mathcal{C}_s$ and unsafe ones $\mathcal{C}_u$.

    \item $\mathcal{E}$ is a set of edges connecting the nodes mentioned above. It captures all function calls involving unsafe callee. Moreover, it tracks the object flows related to these function calls, \textit{i.e.,} the relationships between each dynamic method and its constructors.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}
