Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case1} presents the UPG for Listing~\ref{lst:case}. Note that in UPG, static methods are treated the same as functions, as they can be invoked directly without creating an instance of the struct and therefore do not affect other methods of existing struct objects. However, dynamic methods cannot be invoked without a struct instance. Therefore, we should also consider the relationship between constructors and dynamic methods. Since the objective of a UPG is to track unsafety propagation, we do not need to consider unrelated function calls among safe functions. For any node $n \in \mathcal{F} \cup \mathcal{M} \cup \mathcal{C}$, it can be either a safe or unsafe version. In the following paper, we use the subscript $s$ to denote the safe subset $\mathcal{F}_s \cup \mathcal{M}_s \cup \mathcal{C}_s$ and $u$ to denote the unsafe subset $\mathcal{F}_u \cup \mathcal{M}_u \cup \mathcal{C}_u$. Besides, we employ $\mathcal{C}_l$ to denote literal constructors, which is a special type of safe constructors.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Unsafety property graph generation}
\label{alg:upg}
\LinesNumbered
%\SetAlgoNoLine
\textbf{Input:} $crate$: The source code of a Rust crate \;
\textbf{Output:} $upgs$: Unsafety property graphs of the crate \tcc{a crate may have multiple graphs.}
$upgs \gets \emptyset$ \tcc{Initialize the graphs.}
$callers \gets$ FilterByUnsafeMarker($crate$) \tcc{Locate all unsafe APIs that contain unsafe markers.}
$upgs$.AddNode($callers$) \;
\For{$caller \in callers$} {
    \If{$caller$.IsDynMethod())} { \tcc{the caller is a dynamic method}
        $cons \gets$ FindConstructors($node$) ; \tcc{find all constructors of the method}
        $all\_impls \gets$ GetAllImplementation($caller.struct\_type$) \;
        $api\_set \gets$ GetMutableSelfMethods($all\_impls$) \;
        \For{$con \in cons$} {
            $upgs$.AddObjFlow($con$, $api\_set$) \;
        }
    }
    $callsites \gets$ FindUnsafeCallSites($callers$) \;
    $callees \gets \emptyset$ \; 
    \For{$site \in callsites$} {
        $callee \gets site.callee$ \;
        \If{$callee$.IsMethodofGenericObj() } { \tcc{the callee is a method of a generic-typed object that meets the trait bound.}
            $callees$.Push(FindAllImpls($callee$)) \;
        }
        \ElseIf{$callee$.IsFnParam()} {
            \tcc{the callee is a function variable with a required signature.}
            $sign \gets$ GetFnSign($callee$) \;
            $callees$.Push(FindAllFns($Sign$))
        } 
        \Else {
            $callees$.Push($callee$)
        }
    }
    \For{$callee \in callees$} {
        $upgs$.AddEdge($caller$, $callee$)
    }
}
\end{algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:upg} demonstrates our approach of extracting the UPG from a Rust crate. It begins by identifying all functions and methods with unsafe markers and adds them to the UPG as caller nodes. It's critical to notice the interactions between different dynamic methods. For example, the safety of \texttt{St1::get(\&self, x: usize) -> u8)} in listing~\ref{lst:case}, relies on \texttt{self.ptr} and \texttt{self.len} fields, which can be modified by other APIs such as \texttt{St1::set\_len(\&mut self, l:usize)}. Our algorithm identifies such method dependencies in two phases: (i) filtering methods that take a mutable reference to \texttt{self}, and (ii) constructing an API set for each struct by combining the methods containing unsafe code with the mutable methods identified in the previous step. The algorithm then locates the corresponding constructors and adds object-flow edges between them. Next, it searches for all unsafe callsites within the nodes and adds corresponding function call edges. If a callee is an unsafe dynamic method with a generic instance type, it implies the potential for multiple method implementations. In such cases, the method must belong to a trait that bounds the generic parameter. The algorithm then searches for all implementations of the trait method and adds the corresponding edges. Similarly, if an unsafe callee originates from a function argument, the algorithm considers all possible functions with the same signature in the UPG.

While a UPG can capture the essential components for unsafe code propagation, it may be too large for unsafe code analysis. Therefore, we split a UPG into multiple small units. Each unit is a minimal subgraph to analyze whether the safety requirements of an unsafe API are properly annotated or whether the encapsulation is sound.

Next, we first introduce our approach to obtain the structural patterns of each basic unit and then discuss how to segment a UPG into multiple audit units based on the basic units.

\subsection{Structural Patterns of Basic Units}
