\subsubsection{Patterns with one node} Our analysis begins at each leaf node of the UPG, which corresponds to root unsafe code. A leaf node can be an unsafe function, an unsafe constructor, or a raw pointer dereference, \textit{i.e.}, $f_u \in \mathcal{F}_u \cup \mathcal{C}_u$. Here, we treat raw pointer dereference as a compiler intrinsic function. A leaf node can also be an unsafe method, \textit{i.e.}, $m_u \in \mathcal{M}_u$. These leaf nodes serve as the basis for soundness analysis and must be properly annotated with safety requirements. If the leaf node is an unsafe method $m_u$, its safety requirements may be influenced by the constructor and other methods of the same struct. However, we argue that the safety requirements of $m_u$ should remain independent of them, in accordance with best practices observed in Rustdoc for the standard library. So we merge the two patterns $(c_s\Rightarrow M \circlearrowleft)\ m_u$ and $(c_u \Rightarrow M \circlearrowleft)\ m_u$ that have multiple nodes into one pattern $m_u$, because they both have the same soundness requirement $RS_{m_u}$.

\subsubsection{Patterns with two nodes}: The unsafe node $f_u$ or $m_u$ may have callers. If the caller is a safe function $f'_s\in \mathcal{F}$. It is the responsibility of $f'_s$ to ensure that all the safety requirements of $f_u$ can be satisfied when reaching $f_u$. The soundness requirement is $RS_{f_u} \subseteq VS_{f'_s}$. If the caller is an unsafe function $f'_u\in \mathcal{F}$, $f'_u$ should delegate the safety requirements of $f_u$, \textit{i.e.,} $RS_{f_u}\subseteq RS_{f'_u} \cup VS_{f'_u}$. Similarly, if the callee is an unsafe method $m_u$, we do not need to consider its constructor in the safety requirements. Note that the caller itself may also introduce additional required safety properties beyond those required by the unsafe callees. The subset relationship still applies in such cases.

\subsubsection{Patterns with three$^+$ nodes}: If the caller of $f_u$ or $m_u$ is a method $m'$ rather than a static function, the constructor of the object on which $m'_u$ is invoked must also be considered when checking whether the safety requirements of the unsafe callee are satisfied. Additionally, one or more methods of the object may be invoked before $m'_u$, potentially invalidating or killing the safety guarantees provided by the constructor. We denote such invalidation effects as $KS_M$.

There are four possible combinations of caller and constructor safety, each leading to a distinct requirement for satisfying $RS_{f_u}$ or $RS_{m_u}$.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Safe caller and safe constructor}: Both are responsible for ensuring the callee’s safety,\textit{i.e.,} $RS_{f_u} \subseteq VS_{c'_s} \cup VS_{m'_s} - KS_M $

    \item \textit{Unsafe caller and safe constructor}: The constructor and both verified and required safety of the caller must be considered, \textit{i.e.,} $RS_{f_u} \subseteq VS_{c'_s} \cup RS_{m'_u} \cup VS_{m'_u} - KS_M$

    \item \textit{Safe caller and unsafe constructor}: The caller and both verified and required safety of the constructor must be considered, \textit{i.e.,} $RS_{f_u} \subseteq 
    RS_{c'_u} \cup  VS_{c'_u} \cup VS_{m'_s} - KS_M$.

    \item \textit{Unsafe caller and unsafe constructor}: All four sets must be considered, \textit{i.e.,} $RS_{f_u} \subseteq RS_{c'_u} \cup VS_{c'_u} \cup RS_{m'_u} \cup VS_{m'_u} - KS_M$.
\end{itemize}

Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case2} demonstrates the basic units for the UPG of Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case1}. The UPG can be segmented into three basic units with one node, two units with two nodes, and three units with three$^+$ nodes. The basic units can successfully decouple the complicated relationships between safety requirements and verification duties for the code. 

\subsection{Audit Units}\label{audit units}

While splitting a UPG into basic units can provide guidance for safety property annotation and verification, the units may be too fine-grained and can sometimes be inefficient. Several basic units can be grouped into an audit unit if they meet one of the following two criteria: 
\begin{itemize} 
\item \textit{Two unsafe callees have the same caller:} In this case, the caller should handle the safety properties for both callees. 
\item \textit{The caller is a dynamic method with multiple constructors:} In this case, all constructors must be capable of handling the remaining safety requirements of the unsafe callee that are not fulfilled by the caller.
\end{itemize}

In this way, we can still represent the soundness requirement for each audit unit with one formula. For example, Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case3} demonstrates the final audit units derived from Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case2}. By applying the first criterion, we can merge the two basic units: \texttt{f2->St2::from} and \texttt{f2->St2::set\_len}. The audit formula is $RS_{f_u} \cup RS_{m_u}\subseteq VS_{f_s}$. By applying the second criterion, we can merge the two basic units of \texttt{St2} that contain three$^+$ nodes, and the audit formula is $RS_{f_u} \subseteq ((RS_{c_u} \cup VS_{c_u}) \cap VS_{c_s}) \cup VS_{m_s} - KS_M$. If a data structure contains multiple methods with unsafe calls, there will be multiple audit units.

\subsection{Discussions}

In this section, we discuss additional concerns raised by Rust's type system and justify the completeness of our approach.

\subsubsection{Traits}
Our current approach does not treat traits as a special case because implementing a trait for a struct merely introduces a set of new functions. We can still employ the same approach to generate audit units for the struct, but with more methods introduced by different traits. Such cases are already illustrated in Algorithm~\ref{alg:upg}. Moreover, the new methods provided by traits have little impact on the struct's soundness, as they do not have the privilege to access its private fields and can only invoke existing methods. As long as the struct itself is sound, introducing undefined behavior via traits is impossible without using unsafe code. 

\subsubsection{Overriding}
The next concern is whether a struct can be attacked by adding or overriding methods. Note that Rust does not support method overriding, meaning it is impossible to add a new method to an existing struct or modify an already implemented one. The only way to achieve this is by modifying the original implementation code, which is beyond Rust’s responsibility to ensure safety.

\subsubsection{External Constructors}
Some APIs may return an object of another type, which can be regarded as an external constructor of that type. In our approach, we do not treat it the same as regular constructors when forming an audit unit, because external constructors must ultimately rely on the struct’s native constructors.

In summary, once a struct is proven to be sound, its soundness is preserved regardless of trait implementations or method overrides. Consequently, our audit units are compatible with all potential usages involving structs and traits.

\section{Auditing Rules for Safety Property Annotation}
In this section, we discuss the rules that can be applied to check the safety properties for each audit unit.

\subsection{Auditing Rules}
\subsubsection{Audit Unit with One Single Node}
Such audit units can be regarded as the origin of unsafety because they are not forced to be unsafe by other unsafe code. We define the following rules for such audit units.

\begin{auditrule}[API Annotation Rule]\label{auditrule:annotation}
If the audited unit is an unsafe function $f_u$ or an unsafe method $m_u$, appropriate safety properties $RS_{f_u}$ or $RS_{m_u}$ should be provided.
\end{auditrule}

\subsubsection{Rule for Function Calls}
This section considers the basic function call scenario, where audit units merge basic units with the same caller but different unsafe callees. There are two rules depending on whether the caller is safe or unsafe.

\begin{auditrule}[API Encapsulation Rule]\label{auditrule:call_encap}
If the audited unit contains a safe caller $f_s'$ and multiple unsafe callees $F_u \cup M_u$, the safety properties of these callees should be satisfied by the caller, \textit{i.e.,}
\[
\left( \bigcup\limits_{f_u \in F_u \cup M_u} RS_{f_u} \right) 
\subseteq
VS_{f_s'}
\]
\end{auditrule}

\begin{auditrule}[API Delegation Rule]\label{auditrule:call_deleg}
If the audited unit contains an unsafe caller $f_u'$ and multiple unsafe callees $F_u \cup M_u$, the safety properties of these callees should be delegated or satisfied by the caller, \textit{i.e.,} 
\[
\left( \bigcup\limits_{f_u \in F_u \cup M_u} RS_{f_u} \right)
\subseteq
RS_{f_u'} \cup VS_{f_u'}
\]
\end{auditrule}

Note that satisfying the two rules alone does not guarantee API soundness, as the API may, albeit rarely, introduce additional safety properties beyond those of the unsafe callees. Nevertheless, the two rules remain necessary conditions for ensuring soundness.

\subsubsection{Rule for Structs}
Each audit unit for a struct concerns only one method containing internal unsafe code. If the struct has multiple such methods, we treat them as separate audit units. Since enumerating all possible method combinations within an audit unit is challenging, we define the following weakest rules that must be satisfied; that is, they are necessary but not sufficient for ensuring the soundness of the audit unit.

\begin{auditrule}[Weakest Struct Encapsulation Rule]\label{auditrule:struct_encap}
If the audited unit is a struct with a safe method $m_s$ that contains multiple unsafe callees $F_u\cup M_u$, and multiple constructors $C$, the safety properties of these callees should be satisfied by the method and the constructors,\textit{i.e.,} 

\[
\left( 
  \bigcup\limits_{f_u \in F_u \cup M_u} RS_{f_u} 
\right)
\subseteq
\left( 
  \bigcap\limits_{c \in C} \left( RS_c \cup VS_c \right) 
\right)
\cup
VS_{m_s}
\]

\end{auditrule}

\begin{auditrule}[Weakest Struct Delegation Rule]\label{auditrule:struct_deleg}
If the audited unit is a struct with an unsafe method $m_u$ that contains multiple unsafe callees $F_u\cup M_u$, and multiple constructors $C$, the safety properties of these callees should be satisfied by the method and the constructors,\textit{i.e.,} 
\[
\left( 
  \bigcup\limits_{f_u \in F_u \cup M_u} RS_{f_u} 
\right)
\subseteq
\left( 
  \bigcap\limits_{c \in C} \left( RS_c \cup VS_c \right) 
\right)
\cup RS_{m_u} \cup VS_{m_u}
\]
\end{auditrule}

Rule~\ref{auditrule:struct_encap} and Rule~\ref{auditrule:struct_deleg} do not account for the ensured safety properties invalidated by other methods within the safe audit unit, \textit{i.e.,} $KS_M$ in Figure~\ref{fig:upg-case}. However, these rules remain somewhat complex. We can derive weaker rules that are easier to apply to audit units with multiple constructors.

\begin{auditrule}[Constructor Rule]~\label{auditrule:constructors}
If a struct has multiple constructors $C$, these constructors should meet the following requirement
\[ \forall c_{i}, c_{j}\in C, RS_{c_{i}} \cup VS_{c_{i}} \equiv RS_{c_j} \cup VS_{c_j} \]
\end{auditrule}

Note that in Rule~\ref{auditrule:struct_encap}, \ref{auditrule:struct_deleg}, and \ref{auditrule:constructors}, we do not distinguish between safe and unsafe constructors. If a constructor is safe, then $RS_{c} = \emptyset$. Rule~\ref{auditrule:constructors} can be applied to detect unsound constructors. For example, if a struct contains both an unsafe constructor $c_u$ and a literal constructor $c_l$, the struct is likely to be unsound. The reason is that $VS_{c_l}$ is likely to be $\emptyset$ (not always true), while $RS_{c_u} \neq \emptyset$. However, this is not absolutely correct. We will discuss these issues in the next section.

\subsection{Tag-based Safety Property Analysis}
To verify whether an audit unit satisfies the auditing rules, different techniques can be applied, such as formal verification, model checking, and abstract interpretation. While these approaches have varying strengths and weaknesses, this section presents a new method that leverages the characteristics of safety tags to perform coarse-grained yet effective analysis. Note that these rules are neither sound nor complete, but they are practically useful.

\subsubsection{Set-based Safety Property Representation}
Instead of using the full set of safety tags listed in Table~\ref{tab:sp} to annotate unsafe APIs, our coarse-grained analysis disregards the parameters of each safety tag. As a result, the required safety property for each unsafe API is represented as a set of tags. These tags are in nature a summary of existing safety descriptions in Rustdoc. Consequently, the problem can be modeled as operations on sets. Next, we discuss our approaches to tag analysis. 

\subsubsection{Basic Requirement}
If an API is public and unsafe, it must provide safety property descriptions to users. Therefore, the required safety tags should not be empty.
\begin{infrule} Bottom Annotation Rule: \label{infrule:bottom}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{pub}, \text{unsafe} $}
    \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \neq \emptyset $}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

\subsubsection{Infer Safety Tags by API Signatures and Names}
By analyzing the functionality of Rust APIs in the standard library, we find that certain API signatures can reveal underlying safety properties. Moreover, developers within the same crate tend to follow consistent naming conventions, which can be leveraged to infer appropriate safety tags. In particular, we observe that unsafe APIs in the Rust standard library often adhere to recognizable patterns, as outlined below.

\begin{infrule} Raw Pointer to Ownership \label{infrule:raw2own}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\},\quad \text{ret}(f) = \text{object} $}
    \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Align}, \text{Allocated}, \text{InBound}, \text{Alias}, \text{Owning} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}

    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\},\
    \text{ret}(f) = \text{object} $}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"from\_raw"})$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Align}, \text{Allocated}, \text{InBound}, \text{Alias}, \text{Owning} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

\begin{infrule} Raw Pointer to Reference 
\label{infrule:raw2ref}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\},\
    \text{ret}(f) = \text{reference} $}
    \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Align}, \text{Allocated}, \text{InBound}, \text{Alias} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}

     \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\},\
    \text{ret}(f) = \text{reference} $}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"as\_ref"} || \text{"as\_mut"})$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Align}, \text{Allocated}, \text{InBound}, \text{Alias} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}
 \end{infrule}

 \begin{infrule} Designated Allocator \label{infrule:allocator}
 \small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}, \text{Allocator}\}$}
    \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Allocator} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}

    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{params}(f) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}, \text{Allocator}\}$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"\_in"})$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Allocator} \}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule} 

 \begin{infrule} Unchecked
 \label{infrule:unchecked}
 \small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"unchecked"})$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f \in \text{integer module}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{ValidNum}\}$}
    \end{prooftree}

    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"unchecked"})$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f \in \text{str module}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{ValidString}\}$}
    \end{prooftree}

    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"unchecked"})$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f \in \text{slice module}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{InBound}\}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule} 

 \begin{infrule} Assume Init 
 \label{infrule:init}
 \small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f : \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{name}(f).\text{contains}(\text{"assume\_init"})$}
    \UnaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \{\text{Init}\}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule} 

\begin{itemize}

    \item If an unsafe function takes a raw pointer as a parameter and returns an object, it is highly likely that the function converts the raw pointer into that object, with both pointing to the same memory region. Such APIs generally contain the term ``from\_raw'' in their names and serve as unsafe constructors. There are over 60 such APIs in the standard library. For example, the API  \texttt{Box::from\_raw(raw: *mut T) -> Box<T>} returns an object derived from the raw pointer input. In such cases, the following safety tags should be enforced: \texttt{Align}, \texttt{Allocated}, \texttt{InBound}, \texttt{Owning}, and \texttt{Alias}. 

    \item Similarly, if an unsafe function takes a raw pointer as a parameter and returns a reference, it is likely that the function converts the raw pointer into that reference. Such APIs often include the term ``as\_ref'' or ``as\_mut'' in their names, though this is not always the case. For example, the API \texttt{slice::from\_raw\_parts<'a, T>(data: *const T, len: usize) -> \&'a [T]} returns a reference derived from the raw pointer input. The following safety tags should be enforced: \texttt{Align}, \texttt{Allocated}, \texttt{InBound}, and \texttt{Alias}. 

    \item If a function takes both a raw pointer and an allocator as input, it is likely that the memory pointed to by the raw pointer must have been allocated using the given allocator. Such APIs often contain the term ``\_in'' in their names, typically in combination with ``from\_raw'' as ``from\_raw\_in'', which also serves as an unsafe constructor with raw pointer inputs. For example, the API \texttt{Box::from\_raw\_in(raw: *mut T, alloc: A) -> Box<T, A>} exhibits this behavior. In such cases, we employ a variant of the \texttt{Allocated} tag, namely \texttt{Allocator}, which adds a constraint on the allocator used.

    \item There are about 200 unsafe APIs in the standard library that contain the word ``unchecked'' in their names. These APIs may produce invalid results depending on the module and the return type. For example, unchecked operations in integer modules can lead to arithmetic overflow and should be annotated with \texttt{ValidNum}; Unchecked operations in the \texttt{str} module may result in invalid UTF-8 and should be annotated with \texttt{ValidString}. Similarly, unchecked operations in the \texttt{slice} module bypass boundary checks and should be labeled with \texttt{InBound}.

    \item The term ``assume\_init'' is frequently used in unsafe APIs to convert a value of type \texttt{MaybeUninit<T>} into \texttt{T} or to directly operate on potentially uninitialized data. Such APIs should be annotated with \texttt{Init}.
\end{itemize}


\subsubsection{Safety Tag Analysis For Function Calls}
According to the auditing rules~\ref{auditrule:call_encap} and~\ref{auditrule:call_deleg}, all safety properties of unsafe callees must be either delegated or verified by the caller. We may apply a conservative approach and assume no the verified safety properties by the caller.

\begin{infrule} Delegation Rule for Function calls 
\label{infrule:delegation}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\forall f \in F.\ \Gamma \vdash f: \text{unsafe}, \quad f \Rightarrow RS_{f}$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f' \ \text{invokes all functions in} \ F$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f' \Rightarrow RS \supseteq \bigcup_{f \in F} RS_{f}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

However, this may introduce some false tags due to a conservative analysis approach. We identify several rules that help eliminate unnecessary safety tags.

\begin{infrule} Unchecked-Option Elimination Rules for Function Calls
\label{elim:uncheck_option}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f1: \text{unsafe}, \quad \text{name}(f1).\text{contains}(\text{"unchecked"}),\quad
    f1 \Rightarrow RS_{uck}$} 
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f2 \ \text{invokes} \ f1,\quad \text{ret}(f2) = \text{Option<T>}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f2 \ \Rightarrow RS \setminus RS_{uck}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

\begin{infrule} NonNull-!Null Elimination Rules for Function Calls 
\label{elim:nonnull_null}
\small
    \begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f1 : \text{unsafe} \ \Rightarrow \ RS \supseteq \{\text{!Null}\}$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash f2 \ \text{invokes} \ f1,\quad
    \text{params}(f2) \supseteq \{\text{NonNull<T>}\}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash f2 \Rightarrow RS \setminus \{\text{!Null}\}$}
    \end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

Both rules above filter out irrelevant safety tags based on the signature of the caller.
The first rule states that if the unsafe callee is an unchecked function and the caller returns an \texttt{Option} type, then the safety tags introduced by the unchecked operation can likely be waived. The second rule states that if the unsafe callee is annotated with the \texttt{!Null} tag, this tag can often be eliminated if the caller accepts an argument of type \texttt{NonNull}. These rules are effective for the Rust standard library because functions in the library are typically small, which increases the likelihood that the callee’s inputs are directly derived from the caller’s inputs, and the caller’s outputs are directly based on the callee’s outputs.

\subsubsection{Safety Tag Analysis Among Constructors}
Since the safety property of an unsafe constructor may be related to other methods, inferring the safety tags of the constructor is not straightforward. However, we can leverage the auditing rule~\ref{auditrule:constructors} to examine potential soundness issues by comparing different constructors. In particular, we can first assume that the safety tags of all unsafe constructors should serve as the default safety tags for all constructors, and then apply elimination rules.

\begin{infrule} Constructor Consistency Rule
\label{infrule:constructor}
\begin{prooftree}
\AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{Struct}::c_i: \text{unsafe},\quad c_i\Rightarrow RS_{c_i}$}
\AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{Struct}::c_j \Rightarrow \{VS_{c_j}, RS_{c_j}\}$}
\BinaryInfC{$RS_{c_j} \cup VS_{c_j} \supseteq RS_{c_i}$}
\end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

\begin{infrule} Literal Enum Constructor Elimination Rule
\label{elim:literal}
\small
\begin{prooftree}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{Enum}::c_i : \text{unsafe},\quad \text{name}(c_i).\text{contains}(\text{"unchecked"}),\quad c_i \ \Rightarrow \ RS_{c_i}\supseteq SP_{uck}$}
    \AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash c_l = \text{Enum::Literal},\ c_l \ \Rightarrow \ VS_{c_l}$}
    \BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash c_l \Rightarrow VS_{c_l} \supseteq SP_{uck}$}
\end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

\begin{infrule} Raw Pointer Constructor Elimination Rule
\label{elim:raw_ptr}
\small
\begin{prooftree} 
\AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash \text{Struct}::c_i : \text{unsafe},\quad \text{params}(c_i) \supseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\}, \quad c_i \Rightarrow \quad RS_{c_i}\supseteq SP_{raw}$}
\AxiomC{$\Gamma \vdash c_j,\ \text{params}(c_j) \nsupseteq \{\text{raw ptr}\}$}
\BinaryInfC{$\Gamma \vdash c_j \Rightarrow VS_{c_j} \supseteq SP_{raw}$} 
\end{prooftree}
\end{infrule}

Rule~\ref{elim:literal} states that if a literal constructor belongs to an enumerated type, it cannot simply be considered as verifying nothing, because users can only construct an object using valid variants through the literal constructor. The enumerate type \texttt{ascii::Char} is an example with the literal constructor enabled. It also contains an unsafe constructor, \texttt{ascii::Char::digit\_unchecked(d: u8) -> AsciiChar}, which requires \texttt{ValidNum}. It is impossible to crate an invalid \texttt{Char} via the literal constructor. 
Rule~\ref{elim:raw_ptr} states that if a data structure has multiple constructors, and one of them is unsafe due to raw pointer input, other constructors that do not take raw pointers do not need to specify the safety properties introduced by those raw pointers. For example, \texttt{Box::from\_raw(raw: *mut T) -> Box<T>} is unsafe, whereas \texttt{Box::new(x: T) -> Box<T>} is safe and does not inherit the raw pointer safety requirements.

\section{Experiments with The Rust Standard Library}\label{sec:std-lib}
In this section, we present detailed data from our audit experiment on the Rust standard library. The library consists of three crates, \texttt{core}~\cite{core}, \texttt{alloc}~\cite{alloc}, and \texttt{std}~\cite{std}. \texttt{core} serves as the foundational crate. Both \texttt{alloc} and \texttt{std} rely on \texttt{core}, and \texttt{std} additionally depends on \texttt{alloc}. The target Rust version is nightly 1.84.0, released on 2025-01-07. Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} illustrates the overall framework of our experiments, which consists of four key procedures:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Manually translate the safety descriptions of unsafe APIs from Rustdoc into safety tags.
\item Automatically extract audit units from the standard library's source code.
\item Generate a set of safety tags suggested for each audit unit based on the inference rules. This step uses the manually labeled tags of unsafe callees for inference.
\item Identify inconsistencies between the manual and generated tags, and perform a manual review based on the auditing rules.\end{enumerate}

Since Rust adopts modularized safety, our analysis is mainly restricted to publicly visible APIs. When modules are reused or re-exported, we count the involved APIs only once to avoid redundancy. We next present the characteristics of the Rust standard library identified by our approach, including safety tags and audit units, to demonstrate its applicability. We then describe the discrepancies found in the safety descriptions within Rustdoc.

