\subsection{Characteristics of Audit Units}
We now discuss the structural characteristics of unsafe code usage in these crates.
Following the methodology described in Section~\ref{sec:method}, we extracted the basic units and audit units, with the results summarized in Table~\ref{tab:result-units}. Overall, our analysis yielded 15270 basic units for \texttt{core}, 1905 for \texttt{alloc}, and 464 for \texttt{std}. When considering the number of unsafe APIs, the rate of basic unit generation is relatively high in \texttt{alloc}, followed by \texttt{std}, and then \texttt{core}. Figure~\ref{fig:basic_units} provides detailed information regarding different types of basic units in these crates. Over three-quarters of the basic units in \texttt{alloc} are related to data structures, \textit{i.e.,} they involve a method caller. This explains the high generation rate in \texttt{alloc}, as the number of basic units grows multiplicatively when a data structure has multiple constructors and several methods that internally use unsafe code. In comparison, the rate is about half in \texttt{alloc} and \texttt{std}.
Furthermore, there are significant differences between \texttt{core} and \texttt{std}: 42.5\% of basic units in \texttt{core} exhibit the structural patterns $f_u$ or $m_u$, whereas in \texttt{std}, only 22.63\% of basic units exhibit $f_u$ or $m_u$.
Additionally, over 30\% of basic units in \texttt{std} involve a function caller rather than a method caller. After merging basic units, we obtained 7951, 468, and 335 audit units for \texttt{core}, \texttt{alloc}, and \texttt{std}, respectively. Both \texttt{alloc} and \texttt{core} exhibit substantial reductions from basic units to audit units, primarily achieved by merging different constructors of the same struct.

\subsection{Bugs Found}
When applying the inference rules to detect bugs, we adopt the following procedures:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Apply the bottom annotation rule to unsafe APIs. We identified 181 APIs missing safety descriptions, of which four have been fixed.
    \item Apply inference rules based on function signatures and names. We detected 10 missing safety tags in 6 APIs, all of which have been resolved.
    \item Apply inference rules for function calls. We detected 27 missing or false safety tags in 14 APIs, all of which have been fixed.
    \item Apply inference rules for constructors. We detected one data structure with soundness issues.
\end{enumerate}

Overall, we have fixed the safety descriptions of 23 APIs, involving 49 safety tags. Table~\ref{tab:bugs} lists all these APIs along with the detailed information.

