\subsubsection{Safety Description Missing}~\label{sec:sp mission}
There are 181 public unsafe APIs without safety descriptions in total, of which 177 are located in the \texttt{core::intrinsics} module. These intrinsic APIs are generally annotated with \texttt{\#[rustc\_intrinsic\_must\_be\_overridden]}, indicating that their implementations are not provided within the \texttt{core} crate and are thus declared as unsafe. Although we have reported such issues, they cannot be easily addressed at this stage due to a lack of consensus on how to document their safety requirements, or even whether such documentation is necessary, since these intrinsic APIs are not intended for general developer use. We have finally successfully fixed the safety description for three intrinsic APIs. In addition, we fixed two unsafe APIs named \texttt{str::from\_utf8\_unchecked\_mut}, one is a static function, and the other is a method. The remaining two unsafe APIs without safety descriptions, \texttt{arg} and \texttt{with\_copy}, belong to the module \texttt{ffi::va\_list}. They are experimental methods under an active RFC~\cite{valist-rfc} for supporting variadic functions in Rust. 

\subsubsection{Issues Detected via Signature-based Inference Rules}
Our inference rules flagged 10 defects in safety annotations. Seven of these were detected using Rule~\ref{infrule:raw2own} or Rule~\ref{infrule:raw2ref}, which requires an \texttt{Alias} tag when a raw pointer input is converted into an owned object or a reference. For instance, the API \texttt{CString::from\_raw(ptr: *mut i8) -> CString} constructs a CString based on a raw pointer to a C-style string. However, the safety requirement related to alias was not provided in Rustdoc.
This annotation is necessary because if the raw pointer is later used to access the data while the returned \texttt{CString} object is still alive, it violates the non-exclusive mutability principle. Although no compile-time warnings are issued, this undefined behavior can be detected by Rust’s official checker, Miri~\cite{miri}, as it violates the stacked borrows policy~\cite{jung2019stacked}.

Two issues were detected via Rule~\ref{infrule:allocator}, which requires the \texttt{Allocator} tag for APIs that take both a raw pointer and an allocator as input. The two affected APIs are  \texttt{Box::from\_raw\_in(raw: *mut T, alloc: A) -> Box<T, A>} and \texttt{Box::from\_non\_null\_in(ptr: *const T, alloc: A) -> Box<T, A>}. Both require a safety condition stating that the memory pointed to by the raw pointer must have been previously allocated by the given allocator. Listing~\ref{lst:bug1} illustrates the issue in \texttt{Box::from\_raw\_in(raw: *mut T, alloc: A) -> Box<T, A>} along with the corresponding fix. This issue violates the constructor consistency auditing rule for structs (\textit{i.e.}, Audit Rule~\ref{auditrule:constructors}). For example, another constructor, \texttt{Box::new\_in(x: T, alloc: A) -> Box<T, A>}, ensures the verified safety property of allocator consistency. Note that we do not confirm the issue through auditing rules for function calls. On the one hand, the unsafe callee, \texttt{Unique::new\_unchecked()}, is a private function without safety descriptions. On the other hand, allocator consistency is a newly introduced safety property by the literal constructor, rather than by the unsafe callee. The remaining issue was detected via Rule~\ref{infrule:unchecked}, which applies to the API \texttt{str::from\_boxed\_utf8\_unchecked(v: Box<[u8]>) -> Box<str>}. This API lacks a safety description specifying that the input byte slice must contain valid UTF-8 data. 

