\section{Introduction} %% {{{

Semidefinite Programming (SDP) and Linear Matrix Inequality (LMI) are powerful mathematical tools that are employed to address optimization problems in various domains, such as control theory\cite{fares2001augmented,zhou2005lmi}, signal processing\cite{luo2006introduction}, and machine learning\cite{de2007deploying,kulis2007fast,weinberger2006unsupervised}. These two kinds of problems are intimately linked and frequently utilized in combination, as their conversion is feasible within CVX (another software package for Disciplined Convex Programming) and YALMIP\cite{lfberg2004toolbox,ravat2021lmi}.

SeDuMi (Self-Dual-Minimization) and SDPT3 are both solvers that can be utilized to solve SDP and LMI problems. In this paper, we select these two representative solvers to conduct experiments and compare their efficiency in solving the Stability of Continuous-time Linear System problems.

%% }}} --- Section

\subsection{SeDuMi} %% {{{

SeDuMi is an optimization solver that can be utilized to solve a variety of optimization problems, including linear, quadratic, and semidefinite programming problems. It employs the primal-dual interior-point method to tackle the problem by iteratively solving a sequence of convex optimization problems. The solver starts by solving a relaxed problem that has a simpler structure than the original problem, and then constructs a sequence of intermediate problems that gradually converge to the optimal solution of the original problem\cite{sturm1999using}.

The primal-dual interior-point method consists of two primary phases: the predictor phase and the corrector phase. In the predictor phase, the solver estimates the direction of the optimal solution and computes a new point using a step-size parameter. In the corrector phase, the solver corrects the estimate and computes another new point that is closer to the optimal solution\cite{sturm2002implementation}.

SeDuMi has the capability to handle problems with up to millions of variables and constraints.

%% }}} --- Section


\subsection{SDPT3} %% {{{

SDPT3, also an optimization solver, utilizes the dual interior-point method to solve optimization problems by finding a solution to a sequence of dual problems. In contrast to the primal problem, the dual problem is a lower bound on the optimal value of the primal problem and has a simpler structure.

Similar to SeDuMi, SDPT3 consists of two main phases - the predictor phase and the corrector phase. However, SDPT3 uses the search direction obtained by solving a linear system, rather than using a step-size parameter. While SeDuMi corrects the estimate using a line search, SDPT3 employs a Mehrotra predictor-corrector algorithm instead\cite{toh1999sdpt3}.

Overall, both SeDuMi and SDPT3 are highly efficient and reliable optimization solvers for SDP and LMI. Although they employ different interior-point methods, they share many common features and algorithms. The choice between the two solvers depends on the specific requirements of the optimization problem and the user's familiarity and experience with the software.

%% }}} --- Section

%% ---------------------------
%% A section
%% ---------------------------
\section{Description of the Problem Set} %% {{{

In this paper, the problem is set as the Stability of Continuous-time Linear System.

As we know, the Lyapunov equation $A^{T} P+PA=-Q$ can be stated as

$$
A^{T} P+PA+Q=0
$$
\noindent
for some $Q\succ 0$ and Let $Q=I$.

Then, these conditions can be converted into LMI as
$$
\min  Tr(IP) 
$$
$$
-I-A^{T} P-PA\succeq 0
$$
$$
-I+P\succeq 0
$$
\noindent
where $A$ is a given real square matrix.

The problem set used in this project comprises 42 different scenarios related to the stability of continuous-time linear systems. The scenarios vary in size ($n$) of the given matrix $A$ and in the constraint of dimension ($m$) (i.e., the number of inequality constraint $-I-A^{T} P-PA\succeq 0$ or the number of matrix $A$). The detail of the problem set is shown in Table \ref{tt1}.


\begin{table}[htbp]
\begin{center}

\caption{Different Scenarios of the Problem Set}\label{tt1}
\setlength\tabcolsep{15pt}

\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.4}
\begin{tabular}{c c c c c c c c}
\hline

$n$     & 3 & 5 & 10 & 20 & 30 & 50 & 100 \\ \hline
$m$ & 1 & 3 & 5 & 10 & 20 & 30     \\  
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{center}
\end{table}



\section{Computational Findings} %% {{{

In this section, we conduct a comparative evaluation of the computational efficiency of SeDuMi and SDPT3 for the selected problem set from the following perspectives.

\subsection{Computations Needed (Measured by the Computational Times)} %% {{{

We measured the computational times required by SeDuMi and SDPT3 to solve the problem set\cite{Xu2023}. In Section 3.1, the results presented are all obtained by setting the stopping tolerance uniformly to $1e-8$.

\subsubsection{When $n$ and $m$ are invariant} %% {{{

Through a comparative analysis of various combinations of $m$ and $n$, our findings indicate that the computational times required by SeDuMi and SDPT3 are similar, with SDPT3 being marginally faster than SeDuMi in most cases. However, when the value of $n$ is exceptionally large, the gap between the two algorithms becomes more pronounced, with SDPT3 significantly outperforming SeDuMi in terms of computational efficiency.

The comparison is shown in Figure \ref{fig1}.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{sheet1.pdf}
  \caption{Time comparison of different sets of invariant $n$ and $m$}
  \label{fig1}
\end{figure}

%% }}} --- Section
\subsubsection{When $m$ is fixed with $n$ increasing} %% {{{

As the size $n$ of matrix increases, the computational time of both algorithms shows an upward trend. 

As shown in Figures \ref{fig2} and \ref{fig3}, for small values of $n$ (usually less than 20), the computational times of the two solvers are similar. However, as $n$ increases, SDPT3 outperforms SeDuMi in terms of computational times. The gap between the two solvers becomes more prominent as $n$ grows larger.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{sheet2.pdf}
  \caption{Time comparison when $m$ is fixed to 1 with $n$ increasing}
  \label{fig2}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{sheet3.pdf}
  \caption{Time comparison when $m$ is fixed to 10 with $n$ increasing}
  \label{fig3}
