Our analysis revealed that the performance of SeDuMi and SDPT3 is problem-dependent and exhibits some differences. Specifically, we have reached the following conclusions.

\begin{enumerate} 
    \item In most cases, SDPT3 performs faster than SeDuMi.
    \item Both solvers exhibit an increase in computational time as the size of the given matrix $A$ and the dimension (i.e., $n$ and $m$) increase, but they are significantly more sensitive to changes in $n$ than that in $m$.
    \item SeDuMi is sensitive to tolerance settings, with computational time increasing as the required tolerance decreases, while in contrast, SDPT3 almost maintains consistent computational time. For problems requiring high precision (small tolerance), SDPT3 performs significantly faster than SeDuMi.
    \item The memory usage of SDPT3 and SeDuMi is virtually identical, therefore memory usage is not a significant comparative factor, particularly with today's high-end computing configurations.
\end{enumerate}

In conclusion, both SeDuMi and SDPT3 are efficient solvers for Semi-definite Programming (SDP) and Linear Matrix Inequality (LMI) problems. However, for large-scale or high-precision issues, SDPT3 is proven to be a better choice.

