\cref{tab:centrality} above lists the 10 countries in our network with the highest potential influence according to eigenvector centrality on CRS reports. We observe that many notable global superpowers are included in this list, lending credibility to our algorithm and methodology because it is well known that the US Congress is interested in the activities of these superpowers.

\section{Conclusion}
We apply the Louvain method to a dataset of 2,010 Congressional Research Service reports. By representing 172 countries as nodes and their 4,137 domestic interests as bidirectional weighted edges, we extract and visualize recent international relations. The results we were able to extract, which include an arrangement of countries into 10 distinct clusters and the 10 most influential countries in regards to US affairs, enable us to better understand how countries interact on the global stage. Thus, our tool is able to improve the quality of evidence used for analytic products.

We note that our algorithm serves as a heuristic, and the Louvain method’s inherent greedy nature results in minor variance between different iterations of our results. Additionally, we note that a larger dataset could be used to find more shared interests and increase network density. This higher network density could potentially result in more accurate clustering, as the Louvain method can identify well-defined communities with ease due to the stronger connections between them. It may also be useful to incorporate a method of filtering the dataset by date of publication, enabling the tool to compute results with greater accuracy to a specific time period. We leave this for future work.
% Since denser networks have more cells $A_{ij}$ with nonzero values, $A_{ij} - \frac{k_ik_j}{2m}$ will increase, resulting in a higher modularity score and more meaningful partitions. Thus, it follows that more input data may potentially help extract additional hidden relations.

\section{Reproducibility Statement}
We ensure the reproducibility of our results by making our input data publicly available: \url{https://tinyurl.com/52jsv797}. We then thoroughly explain the process we took when programming our graph tool in the Methodology section, allowing anyone to explore the various clustering patterns the Louvain algorithm may create.
