\begin{comment}


Team semantics offers a unified 
logical framework for studying  concepts that arise in the presence of plurality of data such as dependence and independence. 
The birth of the framework can be traced back to the introduction of dependence logic in the monograph \cite{vaananen07}. In dependence logic, formulas are interpreted in first-order structures by sets of assignments (aka teams).  
Syntactically, dependence logic extends first-order logic by dependence atoms $\dep{\vec{x},\vec{y}}$ expressing that the values of the variables $\vec x$ functionally determine the values of $\vec{y}$ in the team under consideration. 

Independence and inclusion logics \cite{gradel10,galliani12} are further team-based logics that  extend  first-order logic by so-called independence atoms $\pci{\vec{x}}{\vec{y}}{\vec{z}}$ and inclusion atoms $\vec{x} \subseteq \vec{y}$, whose interpretations correspond to that of embedded multivalued and inclusion dependencies in database theory. 
%\miika{If we remove dependence atoms from the paragraph, also other atoms have to be removed.}

Over the past  years, the expressivity and complexity aspects of logics in team semantics have been actively studied. Furthermore, fascinating connections have been established with areas such as  database theory \cite{HannulaKV20,HannulaK16}, verification \cite{GutsfeldMOV22}, real valued computation \cite{abs-2003-00644}, and quantum foundations \cite{AlbertG22,abramsky2021team}.
These studies have focused on logics in the first-order, propositional and modal team semantics, and more recently also in the multiset \cite{DurandHKMV18,GradelW22} and probabilistic settings \cite{HKMV18}. 
The most recent generalisation utilising weighted teams with the weights coming from some semiring $K$ subsumes all the previously defined variants \cite{BarlagHKPV23}. 
\end{comment}



