Let $\KX$ and $\KY$ be $K$-relations over sets of variables $D$ and $D'$, respectively. We say that $\KX$ and $\KY$ are \emph{consistent} if $\KX \upharpoonright (D \cap D') = \KY \upharpoonright (D \cap D')  $. A family of $K$-relations $\pX$ is \emph{locally consistent} if all pairs of $K$-relations from $\pX$ are consistent, and \emph{globally consistent} if there exists a $K$-relation $\KX$ such that $\Vars(\KX)=\bigcup_{\KY\in \pX}\Vars(\KY)$ and $\KX\restr \Vars(\KY)=\KY$ for all $\KY\in \pX$.


