%\jonni{Use contextual K-family instead of Contextual $K$-relation}
\begin{definition}[Contextual $K$-families]
Let $\cons$ be a context set. A \emph{contextual $K$-family} over $\cons$ is a locally consistent set of $K$-relations $\pX$ containing exactly one $K$-relation $\KX$ with domain $C$, for each $C \in \cons$. The $K$-relation of $\pX$ with domain $C$ is referred to as $\pX_C$. % (possibly leaving out the set parentheses of $C$).
%a locally consistent collection of $\Rp$-teams $\pX=\{\pX_\con \mid \con\in \cons, \Vars(\pX_\con)=\con\}$. 
The set $\cons$ is the \emph{context set} of $\pX$, denoted $\Cont(\pX)$. 
\end{definition}
The \emph{support} $\supp(\pX)$ of $\pX$ is defined as $\{\supp(\pX_\con)\mid \con \in \cons\}$. 
A contextual $\B$-family is simply called a \emph{contextual family}, and identified with its support.

\begin{proposition}
   If $\pX$ is a contextual $K$-family, then $\supp(\pX)$ is a contextual family.
\end{proposition}
%\jonni{Note that in most cases, $\charf{\pX}$ is not locally consistent.}
%\miika{This notation only appears here! So took it out.}
If $\pX$ is a contextual family and $\alpha \in K$, we set $\alpha \pX$ as  $\{\alpha\pX_\con \mid \con \in \cons\}$. In contrast to the above, if $\pX$ is a contextual family, $\alpha \pX$ is not necessarily a contextual $K$-family (it may violate local consistency; cf. Ex.~\ref{ex:nosup0}).
