%Note that $\charf{\pX}$ is not necessarily a contextual $K$-family (cf. \Cref{ex:nosup0}).
Let $\pX$ and $\pY$ be contextual $K$-families over a shared context set $\cons$. We set $\pX + \pY$ as $\{\pX_\con + \pY_\con \mid \con \in \cons\}$. Clearly, local consistency is preserved under this operation.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:closedplus}
    If are $\pX$ and $\pY$ are contextual $K$-families over a shared context set $\cons$, then $\pX + \pY$ is a contextual $K$-family.
\end{proposition}
A contextual family $\pX$ is sometimes identified with the set of its assignments $\set{\pX}=\bigcup_{\con\in \cons}\pX_\con$.
Similarly, a contextual $K$-family $\pY$ can be viewed as a single function $\#:\set{\supp(\pY)}\to K_{\neq 0}$,
where $K_{\neq 0}=\{a \in K\mid a\neq 0\}$. %where $S=\set{\supp(\pY)}$.
A contextual family is called \emph{$K$-realisable} if it is the support of some contextual $K$-family.


