For what follows, we treat addition and subtraction of natural numbers $i\in [n]$ modulo \( n \). 
 We call a context set $\cons=\{C_0, \dots ,C_{n-1}\}$, where $n\geq 3$, a \emph{chordless-cycle context set}
 if for all $i,j\in [n]$, the contexts $C_i$ and $C_j$ intersect if and only if $i = j \pm 1$.
 %We note that both context sets $\consfix$ and $\constri$ form such cycles. 
For the remainder of this section, if the context $\cons=\{C_0, \dots ,C_{n-1}\}$ is implicitly understood, we write $\pY_i$ as a shorthand for $\pY_{C_i}$, given a contextual $K$-family $\pY$ over $\cons$. 

Let $\pX$  be a contextual family over a chordless-cycle context set $\cons=\{C_0, \dots ,C_{n-1}\}$. The \emph{overlap projection graph} of $\pX$, denoted $\opgraph{\pX}$, is the digraph s.t.:
%\jonni{Is the empty assignment a vertex? It seems not.}
%\miika{No}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The vertices consists of the restrictions $s\restr (C_i\cap C_{i+1})$, for each $s\in \pX_i$, $i\in [n]$, and
    \item There is a directed edge from $s\restr (C_i\cap C_{i-1})$ to $s\restr (C_i\cap C_{i+1})$, for each $s\in \pX_i$, $i\in [n]$. 
\end{itemize}
