For unary FDs with ternary CDs, the interaction is significantly more intricate. Let us now abbreviate CDs by writing $x$ instead of $\fd{x}{x}$. We have already observed that transitivity can fail: from $\fd{x_1}{x_2}$ and $\fd{x_2}{x_3}$, it does not necessarily follow that $\fd{x_1}{x_3}$. However, if we additionally assume $\fd{c}{x_3}$, $cx_1x_2$, $cx_2x_3$, and $cx_1x_3$, then $\fd{x_1}{x_3}$ does follow.
The next rule  captures this non-trivial interaction between unary FDs and ternary CDs (the above case is its instance with $n = 3$ and $c_i = c$ for all $i \in [n]$).
Note that even the soundness of the rule is non-trivial.
