over contextual families.


We conclude this section with the observation that the contextual transitivity rule is an instance of the contextual chain rule. This implies that reflexivity, augmentation, and contextual chain rule constitute a sound axiomatisation in the contextual setting, that is complete for derivations within a single context. We write $\mathcal{NRA}$ for this set of axioms.
\begin{proposition}
Contextual transitivity rule is an instance of the contextual chain rule.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
It is easy to check that the contextual transitivity rule is obtained from the contextual chain rule by setting $n=3$, $x_1=x$, $x_2=y$, $x_3=z$, $c_1=y=c_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Let $K$ be a positive commutative monoid and $\Sigma\cup\{\phi\}$ a set of FDs and $x$ a set variables that contains all variables that occur in $\Sigma\cup\{\phi\}$. Then $\Sigma\cup\{\fd{x}{x}\}\models_K \phi$ if and only if $\Sigma\cup\{\fd{x}{x}\}\vdash_{\mathcal{NRA}} \phi$.
\end{corollary}


\subsection{Complexity of Derivation}\label{Sec_der}
Next, we turn to the complexity of the entailment or derivability problem. In the classical setting, there is a deterministic linear time algorithm to check whether a given set of FDs entails a given FD \cite{BeeriB79}. In this section, we establish a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether a given unary FD can be derived from a given set of CDs and unary FDs by the use of reflexivity, cycle rule, and contextual chain rule.
It then follows from~\Cref{thm:completeness} that the entailment problem for unary FDs and binary CDs is in polynomial time also in the contextual setting.


% \timon{Better term for ``dummy dependencies''?}
% \miika{I use "contexts" instead of dummies}
\begin{lemma}
    Let $\Sigma$ be a set of CDs and unary FDs and let $x, y$ be variables.
    Then the problem, whether $\fd{x}{y}$ can be derived from $\Sigma$ by a single application of the contextual chain rule (page~\pageref{box:contextual_chain_rule}) can be decided in polynomial time with respect to $\lvert \Sigma \rvert$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The problem can essentially be reduced to reachability in an extended dependency graph of $\Sigma$.
    In order to apply the contextual chain rule for $x$ and $y$, there needs to be a sequence of variables $x_2, \dots, x_{n-1}$ and a sequence of variables $c_1, \dots, c_{n-1}$, such that the conditions of the rule are met.
    This means in particular that there is a ``path'' of FDs from $x$ to $y$ such that each ``edge'' $(x_i, x_{i+1})$ in that path is witnessed by the respective $c_i, c_{i+1}$ in the sense of item~\ref{item:c2} of the contextual chain rule.
    We construct the graph $(V, E)$, where $V$ contains all pairs of variables in FDs in $\Sigma$ and $E$ contains an edge between $a$ and $b$, if there are some $c_a, c_b$ satisfying the items~\ref{item:fd2} and \ref{item:c3} of the contextual chain rule, that witness that edge.
    We then additionally remove edges that do not satisfy item~\ref{item:c3} of that rule and add a start node $s$ and a target node $t$, with edges $(s, (x_1, b))$, for all $b$ and $((x_n, a), t)$ for all $a$.
    Then there exists an $s-t$ path in that graph if and only if a single application of the contextual chain rule yields $\fd{x_1}{x_n}$.

    This algorithm is developed formally in Algorithm~\ref{alg:advanced_chain_rule}\iffull . \else in the appendix.\fi
\end{proof}
\iffull

\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{Algorithm for the advanced chain rule}
    \label{alg:advanced_chain_rule}
    \SetAlgoLined
    \SetKwInOut{Input}{Input}
    \Input{unary FDs $\mathcal{FD}$, CDs $\mathcal{C}$, $x$, $y$}
    $V \gets \{(a, c) \mid a, c \in \Vars \}$ \tcp*{$\bO(n^2)$}
    $C_y \gets \{C \in \mathcal{C} \mid y \in C\}$ \tcp*{$\bO(n)$}
    $W_y \gets \{c \in \Vars \mid \exists C \in C_y : c \in C, \fd{c}{y} \in \mathcal{FD}\}$ \tcp*{$\bO(n^2)$}
    $E_y \gets \{((a, c_1), (b, c_2)) \mid {\fd{a}{b} \in \mathcal{FD}}, \{a, c_1, b\}, \{c_1, b, c_2\} \in \mathcal{C}, c_1, c_2 \in W_y\} \cup \{((a, c), (y, v)) \mid {\fd{a}{y} \in \mathcal{FD}}, \{a, c, y\}, \in \mathcal{C}, c \in W_y\}$ \tcp*{$\bO(n^6)$}
    \ForAll(\tcp*[f]{$\bO(n)$}){$c_1 \in W_y$}{
        \If(\tcp*[f]{$\bO(n)$}){$\{x, c_1, y\} \notin C_y$}{
            \ForAll(\tcp*[f]{$\bO(n^2)$}){$(b, c_2) \in \Vars \times W_y$}{
                $E_y \gets E_y \setminus \{((x, c_1), (b, c_2))\}$\tcp*{$\bO(n^4)$}
            }
        }
    }
    $E_y \gets E_y \cup \{(s, (x, c)) \mid c \in W_y\} \cup \{((y, c), t) \mid c \in W_y\}$ \tcp*{$\bO(n^4)$}
    \eIf(\tcp*[f]{$\bO(n^4)$}){there exists an $s-t$ path in $(V, E_y)$}{
        Accept \tcp*{$\bO(1)$}
    }{
        Reject \tcp*{$\bO(1)$}
    }
\end{algorithm}
\else\fi


\begin{lemma}
    Let $\Sigma$ be a set of CDs and unary functional dependencies and let $x, y$ be variables.
    Then the problem, whether $\fd{x}{y}$ can be derived from $\Sigma$ by a single application of the cycle rule (see page ~\pageref{box:cycle_rule}) can be decided in polynomial time with respect to $\lvert \Sigma \rvert$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Interpret the unary FDs in $\Sigma$ as a graph, where the variables are the nodes and the dependencies are the edges.
    Compute the transitive closure of that graph, then check for all incoming edges $(a, y)$ in that transitive closure, whether $\fd{y}{a} \in \Sigma$.
    If $\fd{y}{a} \in \Sigma$, then accept, else reject.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
    Let $\Sigma$ be a set of CDs and unary functional dependencies.
    The set of all unary FDs that can be derived from $\Sigma$ by using the reflexivity rule, the contextual chain rule and the cycle rule can be computed in polynomial time with respect to $\Sigma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    % Compute the unary derivation closure $\mathrm{DC}$ iteratively as follows:
    In order to compute the unary derivation closure $\mathrm{DC}$ of $\Sigma$, we first compute all subsets of size $3$ of CDs in $\Sigma$ and add the corresponding CDs to $\Sigma$, so that the contextual chain rule can be applied.
    They are derivable by using the reflexivity rule and there are only polynomially many such subsets.

    We then proceed iteratively:
    %
    For all pairs of variables $x, y$, such that $\fd{x}{y} \notin \mathrm{DC}$, check, whether $\mathrm{DC}$ entails $\fd{x}{y}$ by either of the two other rules rules.
    If so, add $\fd{x}{y}$ to $\mathrm{DC}$.
    %
    Repeat this process, until $\mathrm{DC}$ remains unchanged for all remaining variable pairs.

    There are only polynomially many pairs of variables and each rule takes polynomially many steps to verify, so each pass takes polynomial time.
%
    Since there are only polynomially many variables, this derivation closure can be at most polynomially large, implying that it can only change in polynomially many passes in this process.
    Therefore, the entire process takes polynomial time.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Given a set $\Sigma\cup\{\phi\}$ of CDs and unary FDs, it can be decided in polynomial time whether $\phi$ can be derived from $\Sigma$ by using the reflexivity rule, the cycle rule and the contextual chain rule
%    The derivation problem for CDs and unary FDs with respect to the reflexivity rule, the cycle rule and the contextual chain rule is decidable in polynomial time.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
Let $K$ be a positive commutative monoid. Given a set $\Sigma\cup\{\phi\}$ of binary CDs and unary FDs, $\Sigma\models_K \phi$ can be decided in polynomial time.
%The entailment problem for unary FDs is decidable in polynomial time.
\end{corollary}

