The following example illustrates a simple instance of the so-called information causality principle \cite{pawlowski2009information}, which has been proposed as a fundamental rule to explain why certain correlations are not physically realisable.
\emph{Information causality} states that the amount of information a receiver can gain about a sender's data cannot exceed the amount of classical information communicated, even when they share nonlocal resources. This principle is maximally violated by the PR box, and the example presents a propositional dependence logic formula capturing this.

% as well as other devices that exceed Tsirelson's bound, which limits how strongly quantum correlations can violate the CHSH inequality.
\begin{example}[Violation of information causality]
Alice receives two random bits \( p_0, p_1 \), and is allowed to send one bit $m$ to Bob.
Bob receives an index \( i \in \{0,1\} \) and wants to correctly guess \( p_i \) based on the message $m$ received from Alice.
If Alice and Bob are allowed to use a PR box, then Bob is always able to guess \( p_i \) correctly, regardless of which $i$ he receives.  
%Without access to such superquantum correlations, Bob's probability of correctly guessing \( p_i \) is strictly less than 1.
Alice and Bob achieve perfect success when they obey the following strategy:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Alice measures \( a \) if \( p_0 \oplus p_1 = 0 \), or \( a' \) if \( p_0 \oplus p_1 = 1 \), receiving outcome \( x \in \{0,1\} \).

  \item She sends the one-bit message \( m = p_0 \oplus x \) to Bob.

  \item Bob measures \( b \) if \( i = 0 \), or \( b' \) if \( i = 1 \), receiving outcome \( y \in \{0,1\} \).

  \item Bob guesses
    $z = m \oplus y$. The PR box (\Cref{fig:examplePR}) guarantees that $z=p_i$ is always the case.
\end{itemize}
Bob can guess either bit correctly given only one bit from Alice, thus violating the information causality principle.

The experiment can now be encoded as a propositional dependence logic sentence, 
interpreted over the contextual team that forms the PR box (\Cref{fig:examplePR}).
