\begin{abstract}
%\hy{Automated Program Repair with Large Language Models: How Far Are We  ?}
% The increasing prevalence of software bugs has made automated program repair (APR) an important research focus. The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) presents new opportunities for automated program repair. While recent studies have evaluated LLMs' capabilities in APR tasks, most rely on smaller, early-generation LLMs and predominantly focus on Java-language benchmarks. The repair capability of modern, large-scale LLMs across diverse programming languages remains underexplored. To systematically investigate their repair capabilities and identify key influential factors, this paper conducts an empirical study of four representative open-source LLMs -- CodeLlama, LLaMA, StarCoder, and DeepSeek-Coder, spanning varying parameter sizes (7B to 33B), architectural designs, and intended purposes (general-purpose and code-specialized variants). We evaluate these models across 1) two bug repair scenarios (enterprise-grade projects bugs and algorithmic assignments bugs), 2) three programming languages (Java, C/C++ and Python), and 3) four prompt engineering strategies, generating and analyzing over 600,000 patches across six benchmarks. Notable findings from our analysis include: First, while larger LLMs with more parameters generally achieve superior repair performance, the fine-tuned CodeLlama model consistently outperforms the selected LLaMA model across various benchmarks despite having fewer parameters. Second, the number of successful repairs does not scale proportionally with model size; smaller models can also produce unique correct patches. Third, we observe that in the patch-generation phase, correct patches are often generated by LLMs at an early stage. Finally, we find that different prompts have a significant impact on bug repair performance. These insights help establish a methodological framework for integrating LLMs into practical APR workflows, highlighting the importance of model specialization over raw scale, the need for cost-aware verification strategies and systematic prompt optimization. Our findings provide concrete guidance on how to balance repair effectiveness with computational efficiency in real-world LLM-based APR systems.

The increasing prevalence of software bugs has positioned automated program repair (APR) as a critical area of research. The rapid evolution of large language models (LLMs) offers new opportunities for advancing APR techniques. While prior studies have explored LLMs in repair tasks, most have relied on earlier-generation, smaller models and focused predominantly on Java-based benchmarks. The repair capabilities of modern, large-scale LLMs across diverse programming languages and scenarios remain insufficiently examined. To address this gap, we conducted a comprehensive empirical study evaluating four representative open-source LLMs: CodeLlama, LLaMA, StarCoder, and DeepSeek-Coder, spanning a range of model sizes (7B to 33B parameters), architectural designs, and application scopes (general purpose vs. code-specialized). Our evaluation spans (1) two distinct bug scenarios (enterprise-grades and algorithmic assignment bugs), (2) three programming languages (Java, C/C++, and Python), and (3) four prompt engineering strategies, resulting in the generation and analysis of over 600,000 patches across six benchmark datasets. Our findings reveal several key insights. First, although larger models tend to yield better repair performance, the fine-tuned CodeLlama consistently outperforms the general-purpose LLaMA across multiple benchmarks, despite having fewer parameters. Second, successful repair does not scale linearly with model size; smaller models can generate unique correct patches not found in larger models. Third, correct patches are frequently produced in the early stages of patch generation. Finally, prompt design significantly influences repair effectiveness.These findings underscore the value of model specialization over sheer scale, advocate for cost-aware verification mechanisms, and highlight the importance of prompt engineering in LLM-driven APR. Our study provides actionable guidance for building practical, efficient, and effective APR systems using LLMs.

